export const HUB = {"A1": {"pub": {"id": "A1", "title": "\"What's a D20?\" Intro to TTRPG Play", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "5E by default, adaptable in the room", "prereq": "none, and that is the point", "blurb": "A tabletop roleplaying game is a conversation with a referee and some dice, and an hour is enough to learn how a turn works in 5E. For anyone who has never played, including people who have watched a hundred hours of it and still never rolled anything, with no book, no dice and no prior exposure needed. You will leave having actually taken a turn: described what your character does, rolled for it, and found out what happened.", "floor": "They can describe what their character does, roll a d20 when asked, add the right number, and say out loud whether it worked.", "subjects": ["What this actually is", "The dice, physically", "The core loop", "Reading the roll", "When you do not roll", "Live: everyone takes a turn", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**What's a D20? What you actually need.**\n\nThis is 5E. You can sit down at a table knowing only this:\n\n- A tabletop RPG is a conversation with a referee and some dice.\n- The number after the \"d\" is how many sides the die has. `2d6` means roll two six-sided dice and add them.\n- The d20 decides whether something works; the other dice usually decide how much.\n- The loop is: the DM describes, you say what you try, you roll if it is uncertain, the DM says what happens.\n- Say what you want to happen, not what rule you want to use. \"I look behind the tapestry\" beats \"can I make a Perception check.\"\n- Higher is better. You often will not know the target number, and that is on purpose.\n- Most of the game has no dice in it; if you cannot fail and nothing is at stake, you just do it.\n- A natural 20 is not an automatic success at everything; usually it only guarantees a hit on an attack.\n\n**This week:** describe one thing out loud to somebody and decide whether it needs a roll; that is the whole loop, and it works at a kitchen table with one borrowed die.", "sheets": ["A1_Six_Characters.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "What this actually is", "m": 6}, {"n": "The dice, physically", "m": 8}, {"n": "The core loop", "m": 10}, {"n": "Reading the roll", "m": 10}, {"n": "When you do not roll", "m": 6}, {"n": "Live: everyone takes a turn", "m": 15}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["Advantage and disadvantage, in subject 4.", "The percentile die, in subject 2. Mention the other six and move on.", "The third walkthrough of the loop in subject 3. Two is enough.", "Subject 7 down to one line: the name of the next class.", "The hidden target number, in subject 4. Keep higher is better."], "notes": ["The dice reference sheet has two errors on it and it is not being redrawn yet. A dagger is 1d4 and the sheet lists it under the d6, where shortswords belong and daggers do not. The d4 entry's \"when thrown\" misleads, because a dagger is 1d4 thrown and 1d4 in melee; the die does not change with the use. Say both corrections out loud when you reach that slide, and the slide cue says so.", "Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 8 for the first several runs; subject 6 requires every attendee to take a turn, and that is the constraint, not the lecture.", "Pre-made characters exist, six of them, three numbers each, in A1_Six_Characters.pdf and on slide 12; release them to the room at the start of subject 6.", "Expect at least one attendee who has watched a lot of actual play and thinks they already know this. Give them a harder action in subject 6 rather than correcting them.", "Expect at least one who will not speak; call on them by name in subject 6 with an easy, concrete action. Do not call on them before that."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap, and the blurb. The catalog blurb in section 3 of this document is what that blurb is for.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Read the code out, and paste the link in chat. Attendees need nothing else: no account, no install.", "Drive from the teacher view. Arrow keys move, a number jumps, b blacks the screen out for when attention should be on the conversation. The cue under each slide is the line from this brief.", "Release the handout at the end with the handout button, not as a file anyone has to find.", "End the class, then press *Log this run* and answer the three questions below; that is the after-action, and it lives in the room rather than in this document."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "A2": {"pub": {"id": "A2", "title": "Roleplay for Beginners", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. A1 is recommended and is not a gate.", "blurb": "Most people think roleplay means doing an accent while six people watch, and that picture keeps more of them out of the hobby than any rule in any book. For anybody who wants to play but is quietly dreading the talking part, including people who have already played a session or two. You will leave with three questions that make a character playable, and having said one line out loud with no voice required.", "floor": "They can name one thing their character wants and one thing they are afraid of, say one line as that character out loud, and know that third person counts.", "subjects": ["You are already doing it", "Third person counts", "The three questions", "Saying one line", "When you have nothing", "Live: one line each", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Roleplay for Beginners.**\n\n**Roleplay is deciding as somebody else; the voice is optional, and most people never use one.**\n\n**You are already doing it.** \"I look behind the tapestry\" is a decision somebody made, and that somebody was curious or suspicious or stalling. That is roleplay, and it happened without an announcement.\n\n**Third person counts.** Both of these are roleplay, and nobody has to pick one:\n\n| In third person | In first person |\n|---|---|\n| \"My character does not trust him, so she hangs back.\" | \"I do not like this. You go first.\" |\n\n**The thing to stop doing is apologising.** \"Sorry, I am not very good at this\" is the only part of the exchange that slows the table.\n\n**Three questions, and a character is playable.**\n\n1. **What do they want?** Right now, this week.\n2. **What are they afraid of?** Looking stupid is a real fear.\n3. **What do they do when it goes wrong?** This one does the most work and people skip it.\n\n> She wants the debt cleared before her brother finds out. She is afraid of being thought of as the family's problem. When it goes wrong, she gets very calm and very polite.\n\n**Getting a line out, three ways in.** Answer a question as them; ask one in character; or react to something that just happened.\n\n**Short is better than clever.** Four words delivered beats a paragraph thought and not said.\n\n**When you have nothing**, you have four moves: ask the DM something in character, let your character be bad at this, say what they do rather than what they say, or ask a quiet player's character a question.\n\n**Everybody blanks, including people who have played for years.**\n\n**This week:** answer the three questions for a character, in a handful of words each.", "sheets": ["A2_Three_Questions.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "You are already doing it", "m": 7}, {"n": "Third person counts", "m": 8}, {"n": "The three questions", "m": 10}, {"n": "Saying one line", "m": 10}, {"n": "When you have nothing", "m": 8}, {"n": "Live: one line each", "m": 12}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["The second example in each row of subject 2's table.", "Move four in subject 5, which is A4 anyway.", "The worked three-line person in subject 3, keeping the three questions.", "The two short excerpts in subject 4.", "The come-back-at-the-end pass in subject 6, if the room is large."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 10. Subject 6 gives every person a line and twelve lines with pauses is most of fifteen minutes.", "One hard dependency: A2_Three_Questions.pdf, which is new for this class. Release it during the arrival window.", "Say in the first two minutes that nobody will be asked to do a voice. Some of this room came in frightened, and that sentence is the difference between them participating and them watching.", "Do not praise individual lines in subject 6. The moment one line gets called good, every line after it is a performance. A nod and the next name.", "Expect one attendee who wants to do a full accent. Let them, briefly, and route them to B28 rather than holding them up as the standard.", "The six ready-made people on the sheet are deliberately ordinary. Nobody needs a tragic backstory to answer three questions.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice, which is the one subject here that genuinely does."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Say in the posting that no voices are required.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release the three questions sheet in the arrival window, not at the start of subject 6.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "A3": {"pub": {"id": "A3", "title": "High-Risk Play and TPK Avoidance", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. A1 and A10 are recommended and neither is a gate.", "blurb": "Most parties are not killed by a monster being too strong; they are killed in the four minutes before the fight, when nobody asked anything and nobody agreed on a way out. For players who have been in a fight that went badly, or who would rather not find out the hard way. You will leave able to name three signs a fight is above your level, with a one sentence exit plan you can bring to any table, and treating leaving as a move rather than a loss.", "floor": "They can name three signs a fight is above their level, say their party's exit plan out loud in one sentence, and treat leaving as a move rather than as a loss.", "subjects": ["What a TPK is, and what it usually is not", "The signs, before the dice", "The information you did not ask for", "Running is a move", "The exit plan, agreed before you need it", "Live: three rooms", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**High-Risk Play and TPK Avoidance.**\n\n**Most parties are not killed by a fight. They are killed by the four minutes before it**, when nobody asked anything and nobody agreed on an exit. Both of those are free.\n\n**Five signs a fight is above you.**\n\n| What you notice | What it usually means |\n|---|---|\n| **The description goes on** | length tracks importance |\n| **It does not care that you are there** | it is not worried about you |\n| **Your hits do not change anything** | that is information, not bad luck |\n| **Somebody warned you** | a warning is almost never flavour |\n| **The room was prepared** | chains, bones, a cleared floor |\n\n**One sign is worth a question. Two together is worth leaving.**\n\n**Asking is free.** Ask the DM what your character would know; ask the people who live there; ask before you open it rather than after. **One question at a door is worth ten rounds of combat.**\n\n**Running is a move.** Four reasons a party stays when it should not:\n\n1. **Nobody says it first.** Five people privately wanting to leave will stay. Say it.\n2. **We came all this way.** The time is spent either way.\n3. **Somebody is down.** Agree in advance that you drag.\n4. **Nobody knows the way out.** Count the doors on the way in.\n\n**The exit plan, one sentence, agreed in fifteen seconds at the start of a session:**\n\n> **Where we go, what the word is, and who grabs who.**\n\nWhere we go is a named place, because away is not a destination. The word is one word that means we are leaving now. Who grabs who is a pairing, so a retreat does not become an argument.\n\n**Retreat is not a forfeit.** The information is kept, the place is still there next week, and the DM is usually glad to get it back for another evening.\n\n**This week:** agree the one sentence with your party before your next session.", "sheets": []}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "What a TPK is, and what it usually is not", "m": 6}, {"n": "The signs, before the dice", "m": 10}, {"n": "The information you did not ask for", "m": 8}, {"n": "Running is a move", "m": 10}, {"n": "The exit plan, agreed before you need it", "m": 8}, {"n": "Live: three rooms", "m": 13}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["The third room in subject 6, keeping the unsurvivable one.", "The counter-case bullet in subject 2.", "The scouting bullet in subject 3.", "The count-the-doors habit in subject 5.", "The DM-is-usually-glad bullet in subject 4."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 14. Subject 6 is a show of hands and two or three reasons rather than a round of the room, so it holds a larger group than most of the player track.", "One hard dependency: A3_Three_Rooms.pdf, which is new for this class and is yours rather than the room's. Have it printed or open beside you, and release nothing to the attendees until the handout at the close.", "This subject invites war stories and the hour does not survive them. Somebody will want to describe a party wipe in detail. Take thirty seconds, pull the one decision out of it, and move on. Decide in advance how you will say that.", "Nobody is asked whether their own DM was fair. The situations are invented for this reason, and a room that starts judging an absent DM has stopped learning anything.", "Say in subject 1 that this needs no tactical skill. A beginner who thinks survival means good builds will spend the hour feeling behind.", "Subject 4 will produce one person who thinks running is cowardly. Let them say it, agree that it feels that way, and put the question to the room rather than answering it from the front.", "Do not promise that this prevents a TPK. Some fights are unwinnable by design and some dice are bad. The claim is better odds and better decisions, which is true.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice; the three rooms are read out rather than shown."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Say in the posting that it is about decisions rather than tactics.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Keep the three rooms sheet to yourself and release the handout at the close. The exercise in subject 6 only works while the room does not know what is behind each door.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": ["A3_Three_Rooms.pdf"]}}, "A4": {"pub": {"id": "A4", "title": "Table Etiquette and Good Player Behavior", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. A1 is recommended, and A8 pairs with it.", "blurb": "The players who get invited back are not the ones who know the rules or do the voices; they are the ones who turn up, leave room, and are easy to sit with. For anybody new to playing with other people, or about to join a table of strangers for the first time. You will leave able to name the four habits that cost nothing, with a way to handle being talked over, and with the one message that lets you leave a table without burning it.", "floor": "They can name the four things that cost nothing and do most of the work, say out loud what they will do the next time somebody talks over them, and name one thing a DM is holding that a player can take off the stack.", "subjects": ["What a good player actually is", "The four things that cost nothing", "Sharing the room", "Disagreeing well", "What the DM is holding", "Live: six situations", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Table Etiquette and Good Player Behavior.**\n\n**A good player is not a good roleplayer. A good player is somebody the rest of the table is glad showed up.** None of this requires talent, practice, or knowing the rules.\n\n**Four things that cost nothing.** Turn up, or say you cannot; be findable, and answer the group chat. Pay attention when it is not your turn; know your own numbers, which is not the same as knowing the rules.\n\n**Sharing the room.** A session is a fixed amount of attention divided among everybody present.\n\n- **Two sentences, then stop.** The gap you leave is where somebody else goes.\n- **When two of you start at once, stop and say go ahead.** One turn of talking buys the table a norm.\n- **If somebody has not spoken in forty minutes, hand them something concrete**, not an open question.\n\n**Disagreeing well, three different ways.** A rule: say it once, accept the answer, look it up afterwards; a decision: argue for it, and put a clock on yourself. A behaviour: not in front of everybody and not in the moment, and say what happened rather than what kind of person they are.\n\n**Assume the other person wants the evening to go well too.** Acting as though that is true makes it truer.\n\n**What the DM is holding.** At any moment they are tracking the scene, what every player last said, what the NPCs want, a rule they are half sure of, the clock, and whether anybody has gone quiet. **Six things, and one of them is you.**\n\nThat is why being findable and knowing your own numbers matter. Neither is politeness; both take something off a stack that is already full. **You are not responsible for the DM's evening. You are one of six things, and being an easy one is most of what a table needs from you.**\n\n**Leaving a table without burning it is A8**, which teaches the script and the reverse of it.\n\n**This week:** next time two of you start talking at once, stop and say go ahead.", "sheets": ["A4_Situations.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "What a good player actually is", "m": 6}, {"n": "The four things that cost nothing", "m": 8}, {"n": "Sharing the room", "m": 12}, {"n": "Disagreeing well", "m": 9}, {"n": "What the DM is holding", "m": 7}, {"n": "Live: six situations", "m": 12}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 6}], "cuts": ["Two of the six situations in subject 6. Keep both of the ones with a wrong answer.", "The side conversation row in subject 3.", "The feeding-setups bullet in subject 3, which is B11 anyway.", "The decision-clock half of subject 4.", "The six-things list in subject 5, down to its first sentence."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 6 needs enough voices that two or three answers come back per situation without the room waiting.", "One hard dependency: A4_Situations.pdf, which is new for this class. Release it during the arrival window.", "This subject invites venting and the hour does not survive it. Somebody will want to describe a table that went badly. Hear thirty seconds, name where it belongs, and move on: B14 if it was about safety, B16 if it was about a DM, D17 if the person asking is the DM. Decide in advance how you will say that.", "Nobody in the room is asked to describe their own table. The situations are invented for this reason.", "Say in subject 1 that none of this requires talent. A beginner who thinks etiquette means performance will spend the hour worried about the wrong thing.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice; the situations are read out rather than shown."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Say in the posting that it is for people who have not played much.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release the situations in the arrival window, not at the start of subject 6.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "A5": {"pub": {"id": "A5", "title": "Character Creation", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "90 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "5E, 2014 Player's Handbook", "prereq": "none. A1 is recommended and is not a gate.", "blurb": "Character creation stalls because a beginner treats twenty decisions as equally important, when only three of them actually are. For anyone who wants to play and does not yet have a character, including people who have opened the book and closed it again. You will leave with a filled sheet, a name you have said out loud, and one sentence about what that person wants.", "floor": "They leave with a playable character: the numbers filled in correctly, a name, and one sentence about what that person wants.", "subjects": ["What a character actually is", "Picking a class", "The six abilities", "Species", "Background, and the first part that is actually yours", "The numbers that end up on the sheet", "Live: build one, start to finish", "A name, and one sentence"], "handout": "**Character Creation: the order, and the three boxes that are yours.**\n\n**Fill it in this order, not the order on the page.**\n\n1. **Class.** It decides the rest.\n2. **Abilities.** Standard array: 15, 14, 13, 12, 10, 8.\n3. **Species.** Adjusts two numbers; does not decide who you are.\n4. **Background.** Two skills, and where the person starts.\n5. **The derived numbers.** All computed, no decisions.\n6. **Equipment.** Take the class package.\n7. **A name, and one sentence about what they want.**\n\n**The modifier rule.** Subtract 10, halve it, round down. 8 is -1, 10 is +0, 12 is +1, 14 is +2, 16 is +3, 18 is +4. **15 and 14 both give +2, so an odd score is usually a wasted point.**\n\n**What the six abilities govern.** STR is how hard you push. DEX is how precisely you move. CON is how much you can take, and it is never rolled as a check. INT is what you know. WIS is what you notice. CHA is how you land on people.\n\n**The three mistakes everyone makes the first time:**\n\n- Proficiency bonus goes on attack rolls, not on damage.\n- Constitution is a saving throw and a hit point number, never a check.\n- At level 1 your hit points are the maximum of your class's die plus your Constitution modifier; you do not roll.\n\n**If you cannot choose a class:** Fighter, Rogue, or Warlock. Low bookkeeping, obvious things to do on your turn, and none of them is worse than the others.\n\n**If you cannot choose a species:** Human; it is +1 to everything and it is not a worse answer.\n\n> She wants to find out who paid for it, and she is willing to be unpleasant about it.\n\n**One want, in the present tense, that a session could interfere with.** That sentence is what turns the page into a person.\n\n**This week:** say your character's name out loud to somebody, and tell them the one sentence.", "sheets": ["A5_Character_Sheet.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "What a character actually is", "m": 7}, {"n": "Picking a class", "m": 15}, {"n": "The six abilities", "m": 12}, {"n": "Species", "m": 8}, {"n": "Background, and the first part that is actually yours", "m": 10}, {"n": "The numbers that end up on the sheet", "m": 12}, {"n": "Live: build one, start to finish", "m": 20}, {"n": "A name, and one sentence", "m": 6}], "cuts": ["Point buy and rolling, in subject 3. Standard array only.", "The species list in subject 4, down to Human and three others.", "The background feature discussion in subject 5, keep the skills.", "The read-outs in subject 7, down from three to one.", "Subject 1's excerpt pair. Say the floor and move."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 10. Subject 7 is fourteen minutes of one person circulating, and eleven people is too many to reach.", "One hard dependency: A5_Character_Sheet.pdf, the blank simplified sheet, released at the start of subject 7 rather than the official form. The official sheet has about forty boxes a level 1 character never uses.", "Expect at least one person who has already made characters and wants to talk about multiclassing or feats. Neither is in this class; route to B7 and keep moving.", "Expect at least one who freezes on species. Give them Human, out loud, as a recommendation rather than a concession.", "A1 is recommended and not required. Somebody arriving without it can follow this, but the modifier rule in subject 3 will be their first contact with a d20, which tends to show as hesitation at the arithmetic."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap, and the blurb. The catalog blurb in section 3 of this document is what that blurb is for.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Read the code out, and paste the link in chat. Attendees need nothing else: no account, no install.", "Drive from the teacher view. Arrow keys move, a number jumps, b blacks the screen out for when attention should be on the conversation. The cue under each slide is the line from this brief.", "Release the handout at the end with the handout button, not as a file anyone has to find.", "End the class, then press *Log this run* and answer the three questions below; that is the after-action, and it lives in the room rather than in this document."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "A5.1": {"pub": {"id": "A5.1", "title": "Reading Your Character Sheet", "track": "Player 101, module in A5 Character Creation", "level": "Open", "length": "30 minutes, plus a 5 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "5E", "prereq": "none. A5 is the parent and is recommended rather than required.", "blurb": "The standard character sheet has about eighty boxes on it and roughly a dozen come up in a normal session, which is why a new player spends every turn searching. For anybody holding a sheet they did not fill in, or one they did fill in and still cannot read quickly. You will leave able to find any number a DM asks for inside ten seconds, and to say where that number came from.", "floor": "They can take a standard 5E character sheet they did not fill in themselves, find any number a DM asks for inside ten seconds, and say where that number came from.", "subjects": ["The two sheets", "The four regions", "The boxes that confuse everybody", "Live: find five things", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Reading Your Character Sheet.**\n\n**A sheet nobody can read is a wall between a person and the game**, and the standard sheet is harder to read than the character is to play.\n\n**Every box is in one of four regions, which turns eighty boxes into four:**\n\n| Region | What lives there |\n|---|---|\n| Who you are | name, class, level, species, background |\n| What you roll | abilities, modifiers, skills, saves, proficiency |\n| What you can take | hit points, armor class, hit dice, death saves |\n| What you can do | attacks, spells, features, equipment |\n\n**When a DM asks for something, decide which region it is in before you start looking.** That is the difference between four seconds and twenty.\n\n**The eight boxes that confuse everybody:**\n\n- **Proficiency bonus** is one number, added to anything you are proficient in, and it goes up with level.\n- **A filled circle means proficient**, which means add that bonus; an empty circle is a decision, not a gap.\n- **The two numbers by a skill** are the total you roll with and which ability it came from.\n- **Passive Perception** is your Perception without rolling, used when the DM checks whether you notice something.\n- **Hit dice** are what you spend on a short rest to get hit points back, and they are not your hit points.\n- **Death saves** are three successes or three failures, and they only matter at zero.\n- **Armor Class is what they have to beat; hit points are how much you can take.**\n- **Inspiration** is a yes or no box, and most tables forget it exists, so asking about it helps everybody.\n\n**Every number in the second region is an ability modifier plus either nothing or the proficiency bonus.** One rule covers about thirty boxes.\n\n**This week:** have somebody ask you for five things off your sheet and time yourself, deciding the region before you look.", "sheets": ["A5.1_Sample_Sheet.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "The two sheets", "m": 5}, {"n": "The four regions", "m": 8}, {"n": "The boxes that confuse everybody", "m": 9}, {"n": "Live: find five things", "m": 6}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 2}], "cuts": ["The fifth call in subject 4. The first four are the exercise.", "Inspiration and Armor Class against hit points, from subject 3's table.", "The two examples under the region habit in subject 2.", "Subject 1 down to the two sheets on screen and the reassuring line.", "Subject 5 to one sentence, naming A10 only."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first minute, since the arrival window here is five minutes rather than ten.", "Seat cap 16. Nothing here is read aloud by attendees, so the cap is about the chat rather than the clock.", "Release the filled sample sheet at the start, not at the end. Subject 4 does not work without one in front of every person, and somebody playing already should be encouraged to use their own instead.", "A hard dependency, and it is the only one: a filled standard 5E sheet, as a releasable file. A5.1_Sample_Sheet.pdf, built from one of A1's six pre-made characters so the program uses its own material rather than a stranger's.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subjects 2 and 3 survive by voice alone against a sheet everyone is holding."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap, and the blurb.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Read the code out, and paste the link in chat. Attendees need nothing else: no account, no install.", "Release the sample sheet during the arrival window, so nobody is downloading a file at minute six.", "Drive from the teacher view. Arrow keys move, a number jumps, b blacks the screen out; the cue under each slide is the line from this brief.", "Black the screen out during subject 4. People should be looking at a sheet rather than at a slide.", "End the class, then press *Log this run* and answer the three questions below."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "A6": {"pub": {"id": "A6", "title": "Character Goals", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. A5 and A2 are recommended and neither is a gate.", "blurb": "New players are told to write a backstory and nobody tells them what it is for, so they hand over three pages about a burned village and then sit through a campaign in which none of it comes up. For anybody who has written one of those, and for anybody about to. You will leave with a want your character can act on in a single session, a reason it needs the rest of the party, and the three lines that make a DM able to use it.", "floor": "They can write a want their character could act on this session, say why it needs the rest of the party, and hand the whole thing to a DM in three lines.", "subjects": ["A backstory explains, a goal pulls", "Three scales, and the one that does the work", "A want the table can see", "A goal that needs the party", "The three lines you hand over", "Live: write one and trade it", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Character Goals.**\n\n**A backstory explains where somebody came from. A goal is what pulls them through tonight, and only one of the two ever reaches the table.**\n\n> *A history:* \"Her village burned when she was nine.\" *A want:* \"She is trying to find out who gave the order, and she thinks somebody in the garrison knows.\"\n\n**Three scales, and almost everybody writes only the largest.**\n\n| Scale | Looks like | Comes up |\n|---|---|---|\n| **This life** | avenge the village | rarely, and never on its own |\n| **This arc** | find out who gave the order | across a handful of sessions |\n| **This session** | get the garrison roster out of that office | tonight |\n\n**The session goal is the one that gets played**, and it gets replaced regularly, which is the point rather than the work.\n\n**A want the table can see.** Another player should be able to describe your goal without asking you. It is concrete, somebody is in the way, and it can be said in a breath. If it takes a paragraph, it is a backstory wearing different clothes.\n\n**A goal that needs the party.** A want you can satisfy alone leaves five people waiting. Attach it: it needs hands, it needs somebody who is not you, or it points the same way as somebody else's.\n\n**The three lines you hand a DM.**\n\n1. **The want.**\n2. **The person attached to it.**\n3. **Where it could go wrong.**\n\n**The third line is the one people leave out and the one a DM wants most.** Handing over a way for your goal to go badly is permission, and a character who cannot fail at anything is a character nothing can happen to.\n\n**This week:** write a session goal your character could act on at the next table you sit at.", "sheets": ["A6_Goal_Card.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "A backstory explains, a goal pulls", "m": 7}, {"n": "Three scales, and the one that does the work", "m": 10}, {"n": "A want the table can see", "m": 9}, {"n": "A goal that needs the party", "m": 9}, {"n": "The three lines you hand over", "m": 8}, {"n": "Live: write one and trade it", "m": 12}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["The wanting-to-be-left-alone bullet in subject 3.", "The third attachment in subject 4, which is the one people see least readily.", "The let-it-change bullet in subject 5.", "The read-four-out-loud step at the end of subject 6, down to two.", "The two-version reading in subject 1, keeping the spine."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 6 works in pairs and an odd room costs you a partner, so hold the cap even.", "One hard dependency: A6_Goal_Card.pdf, which is new for this class. Release it during the arrival window.", "Somebody will want to read their backstory out. It is the thing they are proud of and the hour does not have room for it. Thank them, ask for the want inside it, and name A6.1. Decide in advance how you will say that.", "The four ready-made characters on the card are deliberately unfinished. Each has a situation and no goal, so the person using one is doing the exercise rather than reading an answer.", "Do not rank the goals in subject 6. The moment one is called the good one, the rest of the room rewrites theirs to match it, and the exercise was about being understood rather than being impressive.", "Expect at least one want that is really a character flaw, such as wanting a drink or wanting to be left alone. That is A7, and say so warmly, because the person has found the other half of the pair on their own.", "Nobody is asked about a real table or a real DM. If somebody describes a DM who ignored their backstory, keep it short and move to what the want would have been.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice; the three scales and the three lines are short enough to dictate."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Say in the posting that a character is useful and is not required.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release the goal card in the arrival window, not at the start of subject 6, so people have read it before they need it.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "A7": {"pub": {"id": "A7", "title": "Character Personality", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. A2 and A6 are recommended and neither is a gate.", "blurb": "Asked to describe a character, most people reach for brave, loyal and sarcastic, and none of those three tells anybody at the table what that character does on a bad night. For players who have a character and want them to be somebody rather than a set of numbers with adjectives attached. You will leave knowing what yours does when a plan falls apart, one small fear that can come up at any table, and what the whole thing is going to cost you.", "floor": "They can name what their character does when something goes wrong, name a small fear that could come up at a table, and say what their personality costs them when it would be easier not to have it.", "subjects": ["Adjectives are not a personality", "What they do when it goes wrong", "What they are afraid of", "It only counts when it costs", "Consistent without being a machine", "Live: the same scene, three ways", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Character Personality.**\n\n**A personality is not a list of adjectives. It is what somebody does when things are not going their way, and it only exists where it costs something.**\n\nBrave, loyal and sarcastic describe a reputation. Three characters can hold all three words and behave nothing like each other.\n\n**What they do when it goes wrong. Five shapes, and almost everybody is one of them.**\n\n| Shape | What it looks like at a table |\n|---|---|\n| **Argue** | relitigates the decision, often after it has been made |\n| **Go quiet** | stops contributing and waits for it to pass |\n| **Joke** | deflects, usually to protect somebody else from the silence |\n| **Leave** | takes themselves out of the scene, physically or otherwise |\n| **Overcommit** | doubles the stake rather than accept the loss |\n\n**None of the five is the good one.** Pick one and let it be the default, so the table can learn to read it.\n\n**What they are afraid of.** Small fears beat dramatic ones. Afraid of spiders is a scene once; afraid of looking stupid is in every conversation for a year. Three that come up constantly: being found out, being left, owing somebody.\n\n**It only counts when it costs.** A trait that never costs anything is decoration.\n\n> *Decoration:* \"She is honest.\" *A character:* \"She told the guard the truth, and now we are paying a fine.\"\n\n**Tell the table in advance what it will cost them.** \"She will not lie to a child\" said in session one is a contract, and a party can plan around a contract. **A cost is not a licence**: the difference between an expensive trait and a derailed evening is whether the table was warned.\n\n**Consistent without being a machine.** The thing that stays the same is the reason rather than the behaviour. Being predictable is not the goal; being legible is. Let the character change, let it take time, and name it once when it happens.\n\n**The check to take home:** if a trait has never cost anything in six sessions, it is not a trait yet.", "sheets": ["A7_Under_Pressure.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "Adjectives are not a personality", "m": 7}, {"n": "What they do when it goes wrong", "m": 10}, {"n": "What they are afraid of", "m": 9}, {"n": "It only counts when it costs", "m": 9}, {"n": "Consistent without being a machine", "m": 8}, {"n": "Live: the same scene, three ways", "m": 12}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["The write-your-own fourth character at the end of subject 6.", "The third fear in subject 3, which is the hardest to play.", "The change-takes-time bullet in subject 5.", "The tell-the-table-in-advance bullet in subject 4.", "The three-word opening in subject 1, going straight to the bad night."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 6 takes two or three answers per character across four rounds, which fills twelve minutes at that size and runs short below eight.", "One hard dependency: A7_Under_Pressure.pdf, which is new for this class. Release it during the arrival window.", "Subject 3 invites real disclosure and the hour does not need any. Say before the question that nobody is being asked about a fear of their own. Decide in advance how you will say that, and say it even if the room seems relaxed.", "If somebody does disclose something real, take it briefly and privately, thank them, and tell whoever runs the program. Same rule as C9.", "Expect the five shapes to be read as a personality test. They are not, and the correction is light: these describe a character rather than the person playing one, and a player can pick a shape they do not have.", "Do not let the room rank the five. Overcommit is the one that gets laughed at and it is the shape that produces the most play.", "Somebody will describe a player who uses \"my character would do that\" as cover. That is subject 4's last bullet and it is also A4 and D17. Name where it belongs and move on.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice; the situation and the three characters are short enough to read out."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Say in the posting that no voices or performance are required.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release the under-pressure sheet in the arrival window, so everybody has the situation in front of them before subject 6 starts.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "A8": {"pub": {"id": "A8", "title": "Finding and Joining a Table", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. A1 and A5 are recommended, not required.", "blurb": "Finding a game takes an evening, and what stops people is writing a message about themselves to a stranger and then sitting through three sessions of a game that is not the one they wanted. For anybody who has learned how to play and has not yet played with anyone. You will leave with an application written and ready to send, three questions to ask before you start, and two sentences for leaving a table that is not a fit.", "floor": "They can send one application to a real table this week, say what they want from a game before anyone asks, and leave a table that is not working without it becoming a story.", "subjects": ["Where tables actually are", "Reading a listing", "The application, written live", "Session zero from the player's chair", "The first session, and what fit means", "Leaving without burning it", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Finding and Joining a Table.**\n\n**Finding a table is a search problem; staying at one is a fit problem.** Most people solve the first and quit on the second.\n\n**Where tables are:** a friend who already plays, a local shop or library night, a big public server or forum, a small community you already belong to, or a paid and scheduled table. The friend route has the highest success rate and gets skipped because it feels like asking a favor.\n\n**Four things a good listing tells you:** when and for how long, how long the campaign runs, what the game is actually about, and who is already at the table. Most listings are missing two of those. Ask.\n\n**Your application is four sentences:**\n\n1. Who you are and how much you have played, stated flatly.\n2. What you liked in a game, or what you are hoping for.\n3. Your real availability, including the weeks you already know you will miss.\n4. One question about their game.\n\nThe fourth sentence does most of the work; a question proves you read the listing, and nothing else in the message does.\n\n**If there is no session zero, ask these three a week before:** What is the tone you are going for? Is there anything off the table content-wise? What do you do when somebody cannot make a session?\n\n**A line is something that does not happen in this game. A veil is something that can happen off screen without being described.** You are allowed to name both.\n\n**Three signals of fit, checkable after one session:** did you get to do the thing you came to do, did the table listen to each other, and could you ask a question without it costing you something.\n\n**Three sessions is the honest test.** First is nerves, second is the real thing, third tells you whether it keeps being the real thing.\n\n**Leaving, in two sentences, sent privately, between sessions:**\n\n> \"Thanks for having me at the table. It turns out this is not quite the game I am looking for, so I am going to step out after this session rather than keep taking a seat.\"\n\nEarly, private, gives a seat back, offers no argument to rebut.\n\n**This week:** send one application to one real table.", "sheets": []}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "Where tables actually are", "m": 8}, {"n": "Reading a listing", "m": 9}, {"n": "The application, written live", "m": 14}, {"n": "Session zero from the player's chair", "m": 10}, {"n": "The first session, and what fit means", "m": 8}, {"n": "Leaving without burning it", "m": 7}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 4}], "cuts": ["The reverse script in subject 6. Keep the first script.", "The red flag and green flag bullets in subject 2. Keep the four questions.", "The three questions block in subject 4, if session zero ran long.", "Subject 5's \"what to do in your own first session\" bullets.", "Two of the five rows in subject 1's table, teacher's choice."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 3 reads work aloud, and above twelve the reading either eats the clock or stops being representative.", "Nothing needs to exist in the world before this class runs. No pre-made characters, no handouts in advance.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 3 is the one that survives being done by voice alone. Move to it.", "A teacher should have one real listing to hand, pulled that week from somewhere public, as the subject 2 example. The invented one is a fallback rather than the plan."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap, and the blurb. The catalog blurb in section 3 of this document is what that blurb is for.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Read the code out, and paste the link in chat. Attendees need nothing else: no account, no install.", "Drive from the teacher view. Arrow keys move, a number jumps, b blacks the screen out for when attention should be on the conversation. The cue under each slide is the line from this brief.", "Black the screen out during subject 3's writing time. Ten minutes of a slide nobody is reading pulls eyes off the page.", "Release the handout at the end with the handout button, not as a file anyone has to find.", "End the class, then press *Log this run* and answer the three questions below."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "A9": {"pub": {"id": "A9", "title": "Player Prep", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. A10 and A5.1 are recommended and neither is a gate.", "blurb": "The difference between a player a table is glad to have and one who slows it down is about five minutes a week, and nobody is ever told those five minutes exist. For anybody who turns up, plays, and has never been asked to do anything in between. You will leave with three numbers written where you can find them, a short list of what your character has promised people, and one intention for the next session that will survive the party going somewhere else.", "floor": "They can name the three numbers they use most and say where those live, name one thing their character said they would do, and walk into a session with one intention rather than a script.", "subjects": ["Five minutes, not homework", "Know your own numbers", "What you said you would do", "One intention, not a script", "Notes that survive the week", "Live: the five minute drill", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Player Prep.**\n\n**The gap between an easy player and a tiring one is about five minutes a week, and almost nobody is told those five minutes exist.** None of it is studying the rules.\n\n**Your three numbers.** The three things you do most, written where you can see them without scrolling.\n\n> Longsword +5, 1d8+3. Dexterity save +4. Second Wind once per rest.\n\n**The test is four seconds.** If somebody asks what you roll and the answer takes forty seconds of hunting, the number is in the wrong place. Three is the right number; five is too many.\n\n**What you said you would do.** Characters make promises constantly and players forget them by Thursday. \"We will come back for her.\" \"I will find out who owns the warehouse.\"\n\n**A forgotten promise is the most common dead hook at a table**, and the DM is usually holding it, waiting. Keep three or four open items, one line each, and say one out loud at the start of a session. Dropping one is a decision; say so and close it cleanly.\n\n**One intention, not a script.** Arrive wanting one thing your character intends to try. A script breaks on contact and an intention survives it, because the party will go somewhere else. Hold it loosely, two is the maximum, and wanting to help with somebody else's thing is a complete intention.\n\n**Notes that survive the week. Three lines beats three pages**, because pages do not get re-read. Write names and one detail, debts in either direction, and open threads the table did not finish. Write it before you close the tab.\n\n**This week:** write your three numbers on something you can see, and one thing your character has promised somebody.", "sheets": ["A9_Five_Minutes.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "Five minutes, not homework", "m": 6}, {"n": "Know your own numbers", "m": 9}, {"n": "What you said you would do", "m": 9}, {"n": "One intention, not a script", "m": 9}, {"n": "Notes that survive the week", "m": 8}, {"n": "Live: the five minute drill", "m": 14}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["The pair comparison in subject 6, going straight to reading out.", "The somebody-else-takes-notes bullet in subject 5.", "The use-somebody-else's-intention bullet in subject 4.", "The dropped-promises bullet in subject 3.", "The worked card in subject 2, keeping the four second test."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 14. Subject 6 is quiet writing and then a handful of readings, so it holds a larger room than most of the track.", "One hard dependency: A9_Five_Minutes.pdf, which is new for this class. Release it during the arrival window, and say that the recap is also printed on it.", "The word prep does half the damage before you start. Say in the first two minutes that nothing here is studying rules, because a room that expects a rules quiz spends the hour braced.", "Expect one person who already keeps extensive notes. They are useful and they are also intimidating to the rest of the room. Ask them for one thing they always write down, rather than for their system.", "Do not let subject 5 become a tools conversation. Which app somebody uses is the least transferable thing in the hour and it will eat ten minutes if allowed.", "Nobody is asked whether their own table is badly run. If somebody describes a DM who drops every hook, keep it short and bring it back to what a player can control.", "The character sheet is not taught here. A room that needs it needs A5.1, and trying to cover both leaves neither done.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice; the recap is read out and the card is three headings."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Say in the posting that it is habits rather than rules.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release the five minutes sheet in the arrival window, so the recap is in front of everybody before subject 6 starts.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "A10": {"pub": {"id": "A10", "title": "Your Turn, Start to Finish", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "5E", "prereq": "none. A1 and A5 are recommended, not required.", "blurb": "A new player is not slow in a fight because combat is complicated, but because they start deciding when their turn arrives and then six people watch them read their sheet. For anybody who has sat at a table and felt a fight get away from them, and for anybody about to play in their first one. You will leave having taken three turns against a real initiative order, with a habit that takes a four minute turn down to twenty seconds.", "floor": "They can take a turn in under twenty seconds, name four things a turn can do other than damage, and say what their reaction is for.", "subjects": ["What a turn actually is", "The action list, shortened", "Deciding before your turn arrives", "What happens when you miss", "Reactions, and the one you already have", "Live: three rounds", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Your Turn, Start to Finish.**\n\n**Your turn is one move and one action.** A bonus action only if something on your sheet gives you one. The reason turns take four minutes is that most people start deciding when their turn arrives.\n\n**You can move, act, and then move again** with whatever speed you have left; almost nobody new knows this.\n\n**Ten actions exist and new players use two:**\n\n| Action | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| Attack | hit something |\n| Cast a Spell | the spell says |\n| Dash | move again, up to your speed |\n| **Disengage** | your movement stops provoking attacks |\n| **Dodge** | attacks against you have disadvantage |\n| **Help** | give an ally advantage on their attack |\n| Hide | a Stealth check, if you have cover |\n| **Ready** | pick a trigger now, act when it happens |\n| Search | look for something, properly |\n| Use an Object | pull a lever, throw a flask, open a door |\n\n**The four in bold turn a bad turn into a useful one.** Help is the one to use more: it costs your action and gives somebody else advantage.\n\n**The habit, and it is the whole class: when the person two places before you starts their turn, pick your plan.** By the time your turn starts you should be saying it, not working it out.\n\n**Have a plan B**, because the board will have changed; plan B is usually one of the four in bold.\n\n**Decide in this order:** what do I want to happen, what gets me there, and what do I roll. The third is the smallest part and the one people start with.\n\n**You will miss about a third of the time.** Four things a turn can do other than damage: move somebody, make the next attack better, stop something happening, or set up the round after.\n\n**A turn judged only by damage is a turn that failed two thirds of the time.**\n\n**You have one reaction, it refreshes at the start of each of your turns, and most rounds you will not spend it.** The one everybody already has is the opportunity attack: when something you can see moves out of your reach, you can swing at it once.\n\n**\"I ready an attack for anything that comes through the door\"** is the most useful sentence to learn here.\n\n**This week:** in your next fight, pick your plan two turns early, and time your turn.", "sheets": ["A10_Combat_Cards.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "What a turn actually is", "m": 8}, {"n": "The action list, shortened", "m": 10}, {"n": "Deciding before your turn arrives", "m": 9}, {"n": "What happens when you miss", "m": 8}, {"n": "Reactions, and the one you already have", "m": 7}, {"n": "Live: three rounds", "m": 14}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 4}], "cuts": ["The fourth round of the practicum in subject 6, if it was added.", "Search and Use an Object, from subject 2's table.", "The reaction on your own sheet bullet in subject 5.", "Subject 4 down to the four things, cutting the framing around them.", "The split movement bullet in subject 1."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 8. Subject 6 gives every person three turns at twenty seconds, and above eight that is more than the clock holds.", "A hard dependency, and it is the only one: the six combat cards, A10_Combat_Cards.pdf, released at the start. They are A1's six characters with an attack, damage, an armour class and one special thing added.", "Release them during the arrival window, so nobody is reading a card at minute twelve.", "The teacher runs the opposition and should decide in advance what it is, because improvising a monster while also running a clock is two jobs.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice alone; the map was always read out loud."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap, and the blurb. The catalog blurb in section 3 of this document is what that blurb is for.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Read the code out, and paste the link in chat. Attendees need nothing else: no account, no install.", "Release the combat cards during the arrival window, and ask people to pick one in chat so nobody is choosing at minute forty.", "Drive from the teacher view. Arrow keys move, a number jumps, b blacks the screen out; the cue under each slide is the line from this brief.", "Leave the initiative order on screen during subject 6, because the person whose turn is coming needs to see it to use the habit.", "Release the handout at the end with the handout button.", "End the class, then press *Log this run* and answer the three questions below."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "A11": {"pub": {"id": "A11", "title": "Playing Online", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. A4 and A10 are recommended and neither is a gate.", "blurb": "Almost everybody finds online play harder than sitting at a table and assumes that is a personal failing, when in fact an online room has taken away peripheral vision, overlap and the body, and every awkward thing about it comes from one of those three. For anybody who is quieter on a call than they are in person. You will leave with a signal you have decided in advance for taking a turn, a way to handle a mixed voice and text table, and one concrete thing to do for whoever has not spoken in half an hour.", "floor": "They can name the three things an online room actually takes away, say out loud how they will signal that they want to speak, and name one thing they will do for a quiet player next session.", "subjects": ["Three things that are missing", "Taking a turn when nobody can see you", "Voice and text are different games", "Attention, and the second screen", "The quiet problem, doubled", "Live: three moments", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Playing Online.**\n\n**Online is not a worse table. It is a table with three things removed**, and every difficulty comes from one of them.\n\n| What is gone | What it did at a table |\n|---|---|\n| **Peripheral vision** | you saw everybody at once, without looking at anybody |\n| **Overlap** | two people start, and half a second sorts it out |\n| **The body** | leaning forward, turning a shoulder, putting a hand up |\n\n**Taking a turn.** At a table you start speaking and the room adjusts. Online that produces a collision, two apologies, and then nobody goes.\n\n**Decide your signal in advance and use the same one every time.** Unmute and say the person's name; a hand; a single word in chat; an agreed reaction. Any of the four works, and using one consistently is what matters.\n\n**When two of you start, stop and say go ahead.** Online the gap is longer, so say it rather than gesturing it. **If you are going to be slow, say so.** \"Give me ten seconds\" keeps the table with you; silence makes them move on.\n\n**Voice and text are different games.** Voice is fast and favours whoever speaks quickest. Text is slower and more equal, and shy players are often strongest in it. **A mixed table is the hard case**, and the repair is on the voice side: somebody reads the chat out, and the table pauses rather than talking over it.\n\n**Attention.** Screen fatigue is real. **Get yourself down to two places to be**, the game and one thing holding your own numbers. Cameras are optional and worth agreeing as a table. Break at the hour, away from the screen.\n\n**The quiet problem, doubled.** A quiet player at a table is visible; online they are a name in a list, and forty minutes can pass. **Hand them something concrete, use their name, and do it once rather than repeatedly.**\n\n**This week:** decide your signal, and use it every time for one session.", "sheets": ["A11_Three_Moments.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "Three things that are missing", "m": 7}, {"n": "Taking a turn when nobody can see you", "m": 10}, {"n": "Voice and text are different games", "m": 9}, {"n": "Attention, and the second screen", "m": 9}, {"n": "The quiet problem, doubled", "m": 8}, {"n": "Live: three moments", "m": 12}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["The third moment in subject 6.", "The mixed-table repair in subject 3.", "The camera bullet in subject 4.", "The do-it-once bullet in subject 5.", "Two of the four signals in subject 2, keeping the name and the chat word."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 14. Subject 2 asks every person for one short line, and fourteen short lines with pauses fits.", "One hard dependency: A11_Three_Moments.pdf, which is new for this class. Release it during the arrival window.", "This class demonstrates itself whether you plan it or not. The collisions in subject 2 will happen live. Name them when they do, warmly and once, because it lands harder than the bullet does.", "Do not let this become a software conversation. Somebody will ask which platform handles this best, and that is A14. Name it and move on, because it will otherwise eat ten minutes.", "Expect somebody who says they are simply bad at online play. Subject 1 exists for them, so take that answer in subject 1 rather than deflecting it.", "Nobody is asked about a real table. The three moments are invented and the room is told so.", "Asking the second moment in chat only is deliberate and it works. Do not explain it beforehand or the effect goes.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice; the moments are read out, which is how two of them run anyway."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Say in the posting that it is about playing rather than about software.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release the three moments in the arrival window, not at the start of subject 6.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "A12": {"pub": {"id": "A12", "title": "Teaching Someone Else to Play", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. Having played once is the only real one, and it is not checked.", "blurb": "Almost everybody teaches the game the way they were taught it, with a long explanation and then a short game, and the explanation is where the new person quietly decides this is not for them. For anybody who has tried to bring a friend in and watched it not take. You will leave able to run twenty minutes for one person without explaining anything first, with the three things that go on a page instead of a character sheet, and with a better answer to \"what can I do?\" than a list.", "floor": "They can run twenty minutes for one person without explaining anything first, name the three things that go on the page instead of a character sheet, and answer \"what can I do?\" without giving a list.", "subjects": ["What actually stops people", "The twenty minute first session", "What to leave out", "Answering \"what can I do?\"", "The second session problem", "Live: twenty minutes, in pairs", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Teaching Someone Else to Play.**\n\n**Teaching somebody to play is not explaining the game. It is running twenty minutes in which they decide something and find out what happened.**\n\n**The real barrier is not complexity.** It is the fear of being the one at the table who does not know, and an explanation makes that worse, because it is a list of things they are now responsible for knowing.\n\n**The twenty minute first session.** One person, one scene, opened in the middle of something.\n\n1. **A situation with an obvious problem.** A locked room, a person who will not answer.\n2. **They say what they try**, and it works or it does not.\n3. **One roll, when it is actually uncertain**, explained in one sentence at the moment it happens.\n4. **A consequence they caused.** Something is different because of what they did.\n\n**Stop while they still want more.** Twenty minutes ending early is a person asking when the next one is.\n\n**Leave out character creation, the setting, and the rules.** Three things go on a piece of paper instead: a name, one thing they are good at in plain words, and one thing they have. That is a character and it is enough.\n\n**\"What can I do?\" The wrong answer is the list.** It teaches them the game is a menu to memorise.\n\n> **The right answer is a question: what do you want to happen?**\n\nThen take whatever they say seriously. It takes two or three rounds before somebody believes you. **The game runs on intentions rather than on options**, which is what experienced players know and nobody says out loud.\n\n**The second session problem.** Most people who enjoy a first session never play again, and it is almost never because they did not enjoy it. **Book the next one before the first one ends**, with a day attached. Make it smaller than they expect rather than bigger, and do not hand them to a table of five fluent strangers yet.\n\n**This week:** run twenty minutes for one person, and explain nothing before you start.", "sheets": ["A12_First_Twenty.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "What actually stops people", "m": 7}, {"n": "The twenty minute first session", "m": 10}, {"n": "What to leave out", "m": 9}, {"n": "Answering \"what can I do?\"", "m": 9}, {"n": "The second session problem", "m": 8}, {"n": "Live: twenty minutes, in pairs", "m": 12}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["The introduce-one-person-at-a-time bullet in subject 5.", "The combat exception in subject 4, which is A10 anyway.", "The give-them-the-book-after bullet in subject 3.", "The second swap in subject 6, running one direction only.", "The four-step shape in subject 2, down to its first and last steps."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 6 works in pairs and an odd room costs you a partner, so hold the cap even.", "One hard dependency: A12_First_Twenty.pdf, which is new for this class. Release it during the arrival window and tell the room that the person playing should not read the scene.", "This hour contradicts how most of the room was taught, and some of them will push back in subject 2. Let the argument happen for a minute and then put it to the practicum, which settles it better than you can.", "Somebody will say their group needs the rules explained because the system is complicated. That may be true of their system and it is not true of twenty minutes. Agree about their system and keep the scope.", "Do not let subject 3 become a system-recommendation conversation. Which rules-light game is best will eat ten minutes, and the hour works in any system, including whatever heavy one they already own.", "Nobody is asked to describe a friend who would not play. If somebody offers one, keep it short; the story is usually about a person rather than about a technique.", "Say explicitly that nobody here is being told to become a DM. The close mentions C1 once, and a room that hears a recruitment pitch stops listening.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice; the scene is read out and the pairs work in breakout or in chat."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Say in the posting that it is for players rather than for DMs.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release the scene in the arrival window, with the instruction that whoever plays should not read it.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "A13": {"pub": {"id": "A13", "title": "Surviving Traps and Solving Puzzles", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. A10 and A3 are recommended and neither is a gate.", "blurb": "Nobody needs convincing that traps are dangerous, and everybody has sat through the forty minutes where a table argues about a door. For players who have lost an evening to a corridor, a riddle, or both. You will leave able to say what a trap is actually costing you, search a room by naming where and why rather than by ritual, and put a clock on a puzzle before the table has lost twenty minutes to it.", "floor": "They can say what a trap is costing them rather than only what it does, search a room by naming where and why instead of by ritual, and put a clock on a puzzle before the table has lost twenty minutes to it.", "subjects": ["What a trap is actually for", "Searching without the ritual", "When you find one", "Puzzles eat evenings", "Solving it as a table", "Live: a corridor and a door", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Surviving Traps and Solving Puzzles.**\n\n**A trap punishes a wrong guess, so parties take it seriously. A puzzle punishes nothing, so parties never stop.** That one asymmetry explains most of what goes wrong with both.\n\n**Three kinds of trap, and the kind decides the answer.**\n\n| Kind | What it is for | What it costs you |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A toll** | making the corridor expensive | hit points, a resource, a spell |\n| **An alarm** | telling somebody you are here | the element of surprise |\n| **A gate** | making you solve it or go round | time, or another route |\n\nA toll can be paid and an alarm can be accepted. **Only a gate has to be dealt with.**\n\n**Searching without the ritual.** \"I check for traps\" every ten feet is the slowest way to play. Say where and why instead: \"I look at the floor in front of the door, because the dust stops there.\" That is a question a DM can answer.\n\n**When you find one, four moves, and disarming is the worst of them.** Go round, because a trap on a door is not a trap on a wall. Set it off safely, from a distance or with the door shut. Pay it, if the toll is small. Disarm it last.\n\n**Puzzles eat evenings.** A puzzle costs nothing to guess at, so there is no pressure, so a table circles it.\n\n- **Put a clock on it, out loud, early.** \"Ten minutes, then we try something or we leave it.\"\n- **Decide what happens when it runs out, before it runs out.** A clock with no consequence is not a clock.\n- **Leaving a puzzle unsolved is a legitimate ending.**\n\n**Three roles beat six people thinking out loud.** Somebody writes what the puzzle says and what has been tried; somebody keeps the clock; somebody asks the DM to repeat the description. Half of all puzzle arguments are two people working from different descriptions.\n\n**Say your idea once, completely, then stop.** Repeating it louder is what the forty minutes is made of.\n\n**This week:** the next time your table stalls on something, say how long you have been on it.", "sheets": ["A13_Corridor.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "What a trap is actually for", "m": 7}, {"n": "Searching without the ritual", "m": 9}, {"n": "When you find one", "m": 8}, {"n": "Puzzles eat evenings", "m": 10}, {"n": "Solving it as a table", "m": 9}, {"n": "Live: a corridor and a door", "m": 12}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["The two-places-always-worth-a-look bullet in subject 2.", "The write-down-wrong-answers bullet in subject 5.", "The trap-already-found bullet in subject 3.", "The puzzle-with-a-cost bullet in subject 4.", "The corridor half of subject 6, keeping the door and the clock."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 6 is a whole-room conversation twice and above twelve the quiet half stops contributing to it.", "One hard dependency: A13_Corridor.pdf, which is new for this class. Release it during the arrival window and tell the room the door's answer is not on it.", "Have a real timer and let the room hear it. A teacher saying \"time\" is a suggestion; a timer going off is the lesson, and the difference is larger than it sounds.", "Both answers are written out above, under their own heading. Have them in front of you, because improvising a riddle answer in the room is how a teaching point becomes a trick.", "Expect somebody who is very good at puzzles and wants to solve it fast. That is fine and it is not the exercise. Thank them and ask what they would do if the table disagreed with them, which is the actual subject.", "Do not let subject 3 become a rules conversation about disarming. The point is that three cheaper moves exist, and the specific mechanics belong to whatever system the table plays.", "Nobody is asked about a DM who ran a bad puzzle. If somebody offers a story, take the one decision out of it and move on.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice; the corridor and the door are both read aloud anyway."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Say in the posting that half of it is about puzzles rather than traps.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release the corridor sheet in the arrival window, and have a timer ready before subject 6.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "**The room does not have these and you need both.** The sheet carries the corridor's description and the door's riddle; neither answer is printed on it.\n\n**The corridor.** The dust is banked against both walls and swept clear down the middle, which is where people walk. The line crossing the floor two thirds along is the trigger, and the drag mark is what set it off last time. **Looking at the floor finds it. The cheap answers are stepping over it or setting it off from a distance with something heavy**, and both beat disarming, which is what the room will suggest first.\n\n**The door.** Four dials, four seasons.\n\n> \"I am spent before I am counted, and the first of us is the last you will see.\"\n\n**The answer is the seasons in reverse: winter, autumn, summer, spring.** A year is spent before anybody counts it, and spring, the first of the four, goes on the last dial. **The warm frame is atmosphere rather than a clue**, and if somebody chases it, say so rather than letting them spend the clock on it.\n\n**Give both answers at the end**, after the conversation about stopping. Withholding them turns a teaching point into a trick.", "sheets": []}}, "A14": {"pub": {"id": "A14", "title": "Online Tables: Kinspire and Elsewhere", "track": "Player 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. A11 pairs with it and is not a gate.", "blurb": "Somebody who has only ever used one virtual table has learned a product rather than a skill, and a wall of unfamiliar software is the slowest possible way into a new one. For players joining an online table for the first time, or moving to a platform they have never seen. You will leave able to name the four things every virtual table has, set yourself up on an unfamiliar one in about five minutes, and handle a dropped connection without costing the table ten minutes.", "floor": "They can name the four things every virtual table has, set themselves up on an unfamiliar platform in about five minutes, and say what they will do in the first thirty seconds after their connection drops.", "subjects": ["A shared document, not a video call", "The four things every table has", "Your own setup, in five minutes", "Driving without slowing the table", "When the connection goes", "Live: a cold platform", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Online Tables: Kinspire and Elsewhere.**\n\n**A virtual table is a shared document that several people are editing at once.** Every one of them has the same four things, and the rest is where they put them.\n\n| The thing | What it is for | What it is called elsewhere |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A map, with tokens** | where everybody is, and what is between you | board, canvas, scene, battlemap |\n| **Dice** | rolling where the table can see it | roller, tray, command line |\n| **A chat** | text, whispers, and the log of what happened | log, journal, messages |\n| **Handouts** | what the DM hands you mid-scene | documents, assets, library, pins |\n\n**Find those four and you can play on it.** The names change and the thing does not.\n\n**Your own setup, five minutes, before the session.** Roll a die where the table can see it. Move your own token, and find out whether you can. Send one message and find whispers. Open a handout. Then set your name to something the table recognises, and join ten minutes early the first time.\n\n**Driving without slowing the table.** The cost of a platform is not learning it, it is hunting during somebody else's scene.\n\n- **Keep your three most-used things somewhere that is not four clicks deep.**\n- **Roll and move on your own turn.** Pre-moving during somebody else's scene is the commonest small irritation on a virtual table.\n- **Ask in chat rather than interrupting**, and the DM answers in the next gap.\n- **If you cannot find something, say so in one sentence and move on.**\n\n**When the connection goes. Rejoin first, explain second.** Know where the fallback is before you need it, and make sure it does not depend on the platform being up. If you are gone more than a few minutes, the table should already have agreed whether somebody plays your character or skips you.\n\n**If the platform itself goes down, the session does not have to.**\n\n**This week:** open whatever your table uses, roll one die, and find where handouts arrive.", "sheets": ["A14_Cold_Platform.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "A shared document, not a video call", "m": 7}, {"n": "The four things every table has", "m": 10}, {"n": "Your own setup, in five minutes", "m": 9}, {"n": "Driving without slowing the table", "m": 9}, {"n": "When the connection goes", "m": 8}, {"n": "Live: a cold platform", "m": 12}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["The two optional things in subject 2, keeping the four.", "The platform-is-down bullet in subject 5.", "The setup-list half of subject 6.", "The ask-in-chat bullet in subject 4.", "The go-round-the-room in subject 2, if the room is large."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes, and this class should say why that link exists rather than just posting it.", "Seat cap 14. Subject 6 works alone or in pairs and the discussion afterwards is a show of answers rather than a round.", "One hard dependency: A14_Cold_Platform.pdf, which is new for this class. Release it during the arrival window.", "Do not give a tour of Kinspire. Subject 1 shows the four things and stops, and a tour turns a transferable hour into product training.", "Do not let this become a which-platform-is-best conversation. It will if allowed and it helps nobody; the hour works on all of them and says so.", "Expect one attendee who is fluent in one platform and dismissive of the others. They are useful in subject 2 and a problem in subject 6. Ask them to sit out the first four minutes and then take their answer last.", "Somebody will ask about who is quiet or how to take a turn. That is A11, and naming it takes four seconds.", "Nobody is asked to share a screen or to demonstrate anything. The screenshot is on the sheet for this reason.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice only if everybody has the sheet, which is why it is released in the arrival window rather than at the start of the subject."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Say in the posting that it covers other platforms as well as this one.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release the cold platform sheet in the arrival window, so everybody has it before subject 6 and before anything breaks.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "B25": {"pub": {"id": "B25", "title": "Playing Outside 5E", "track": "Player 102", "level": "Seated", "length": "75 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "many, by design", "prereq": "none. A1 is recommended, and this is where A1 sends anybody who plays something else.", "blurb": "An unfamiliar system looks like a wall and is usually three answers in a different order, which means reading a strange game is a skill rather than a memory test. For players curious about what else is out there, or invited to a game of something they have never played. You will leave having read a system nobody in the room had seen before, and able to say what it rewards and what it would cost you.", "floor": "They can open a rulebook they have never seen, find the three things that decide how it plays, and say out loud which of two unfamiliar games they would rather sit down at and what it would cost them.", "subjects": ["What you already know", "What else is out there", "The three things that decide how a game plays", "Reading a rulebook you have never seen", "What does not transfer", "Live: ten minutes with a strange game", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Playing Outside 5E.**\n\n**Most of what you already know transfers.** You say what your character does, something settles whether it works, and the result changes what happens next. Nearly every system runs that loop.\n\n**The three questions. Ten minutes, and you can sit down at the table.**\n\n1. **What do I roll, and what am I trying to beat?**\n2. **What happens when I fail?** Nothing and retry, failure plus a worsening, or success at a cost; this changes a game more than the dice do.\n3. **What does the game expect me to spend?** If it gives you something to spend, it expects you to run out.\n\n**Reading a rulebook you have never seen, in this order.** The example of play; the character sheet, because what a game tracks is what it is about. The core roll. One round of the main activity. **Skip the setting on a first pass.**\n\n| Family | How it settles the middle step |\n|---|---|\n| d20 and a modifier | one die against a target number |\n| Dice pools | roll several, count successes |\n| Two d6 and a stat | one roll, three bands of result |\n| Percentile and roll-under | roll low against a skill number |\n| Diceless | spending, bidding, or agreement |\n| Solo and GM-less | oracles, prompts, or shared authority |\n\n**Four things that do not transfer, and three of them fail quietly.** Higher is better is false in a roll-under system. My turn is not universal, because some games pass the spotlight; hit points are not universal, and going down may not mean dying. Who decides is not universal.\n\n**The repair for all four is one sentence**, said at the table: I have not played this before, so tell me what to do when I am not sure.\n\n**This week:** open a rulebook you have never read and answer the three questions in ten minutes.", "sheets": ["B25_Strange_Game.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "What you already know", "m": 6}, {"n": "What else is out there", "m": 14}, {"n": "The three things that decide how a game plays", "m": 12}, {"n": "Reading a rulebook you have never seen", "m": 12}, {"n": "What does not transfer", "m": 10}, {"n": "Live: ten minutes with a strange game", "m": 16}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["Two families from subject 2, named on the slide and not spoken.", "The structural families in subject 2, down to one sentence.", "The last two places in subject 4, keeping the example of play and the sheet.", "The share stage of subject 6, down to two readers.", "The fourth item in subject 5, which is who decides."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 6 needs four readings in six minutes and a room small enough that listening to them is not passive.", "One hard dependency: B25_Strange_Game.pdf, which is new for this class. Release it during the arrival window, and say it is invented, so nobody spends the hour trying to place it.", "Expect system loyalty in subject 2 and head it off by asking what a family is for rather than which is best.", "At least one person in the room has tried to read a rulebook front to back and stopped. Subject 4 is for them, and saying so out loud is most of the work.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice. The page is read out in sections and the room answers together, which is also cut list item 4."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Say in the posting that no particular system is assumed.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release the practicum page in the arrival window, not at the start of subject 6.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "C1": {"pub": {"id": "C1", "title": "\"Know your Limits\" Intro to DMing 101", "track": "DM 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "5E by default, adaptable in the room", "prereq": "none", "blurb": "Most people who want to run a game never do, and it is almost always because of an imagined job description rather than the real one. For anyone who has thought about DMing and has not started, and for anyone who started and is not sure they are allowed to be this unprepared. You will leave having made a ruling out loud, with an opening you can use on Friday and a short list of things you are allowed to not know.", "floor": "They can open a session, make a ruling they are not sure about, and keep the game moving without stopping to look anything up.", "subjects": ["What a DM actually does", "The limits, named out loud", "Say yes, or say roll", "The first ten minutes", "When it goes wrong at the table", "Live: three rulings and an opening", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**\"Know your Limits\": what a DM actually has to do.**\n\n**You do not need to know everything; you need to know what you are allowed to not know.**\n\n**The job is three things:** describe the situation, decide what happens when nobody can predict it, and play everyone who is not a player character. Being fair means being consistent, not being even.\n\n**The six rules that are load-bearing:**\n\n1. Roll a d20, add a modifier, compare it to a number.\n2. You pick the number; easy 10, normal 15, hard 20.\n3. An attack roll compares to Armor Class instead.\n4. Damage is the die the weapon or spell names, plus a modifier.\n5. A saving throw is the same d20 roll, made by whoever is resisting.\n6. Advantage keeps the higher of two d20s; disadvantage keeps the lower.\n\n**Say yes, or say roll.** If the outcome is uncertain and failing would matter, they roll; otherwise it just happens. \"No\" is almost never the answer; \"yes, and here is what it costs\" almost always is.\n\n**Pick the number in two seconds and move on.** If you got it wrong, keep going anyway; consistency inside a session beats correctness across many.\n\n**Open every scene in four lines:** where they are, one thing happening, one person who wants something, and a question aimed at one player by name.\n\n> \"You are in the Drowned Crow, and it is too quiet for a Friday. The innkeeper has been watching the door since you sat down. Mara, you are the one facing it. What do you see?\"\n\n**\"I do not know; let us find out.\" costs you nothing.** Say it once early on purpose.\n\n**This week:** run something; a one-shot, three people, ninety minutes; two weeks from now this class has evaporated.", "sheets": []}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "What a DM actually does", "m": 7}, {"n": "The limits, named out loud", "m": 10}, {"n": "Say yes, or say roll", "m": 10}, {"n": "The first ten minutes", "m": 10}, {"n": "When it goes wrong at the table", "m": 8}, {"n": "Live: three rulings and an opening", "m": 12}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 3}], "cuts": ["The reusable note at the end of subject 4.", "The \"limits that are real\" bullets in subject 2, keep the bolded distinction.", "The volunteer opening in subject 6, down from five minutes to two.", "The two stalling and moving excerpts in subject 3, keep the 10/15/20 line.", "Subject 5 reduced to the four headline moves with no elaboration."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 6 needs every person to speak, and twelve rulings at ten seconds plus reaction time is already most of seven minutes.", "Somebody who has never played at all should be routed to A1 first, and should not be turned away. They can take this class and get something from it; they will get more from it afterward. Say it as a recommendation, never as a door.", "The three situations and the premise in subject 6 are invented. Keep them, rewrite them, or swap in three of your own; what matters is that they stop changing after the first run.", "Expect at least one attendee who has already run games and came for permission rather than technique; the subject 2 material is for them as much as for the beginners."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap, and the blurb. The catalog blurb in section 3 of this document is what that blurb is for.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Read the code out, and paste the link in chat. Attendees need nothing else: no account, no install.", "Drive from the teacher view. Arrow keys move, a number jumps, b blacks the screen out for when attention should be on the conversation. The cue under each slide is the line from this brief.", "Release the handout at the end with the handout button, not as a file anyone has to find.", "End the class, then press *Log this run* and answer the three questions below; that is the after-action, and it lives in the room rather than in this document."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "C2": {"pub": {"id": "C2", "title": "Prep and Atmosphere", "track": "DM 101", "level": "Open", "length": "75 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none", "blurb": "Prep paralysis ends more new DM careers than bad sessions do, and most sessions that feel flat were decided before anyone sat down. For anyone who has run a game or is about to, and found the preparing harder than the running. You will leave with a filled one-page prep sheet and three sensory details ready for your next opening scene.", "floor": "They can prep a session in under an hour on a one-page sheet, and name the three sensory details they will open the first scene with.", "subjects": ["The join", "What actually needs prepping", "The one-page prep sheet", "Atmosphere is three details, not a paragraph", "Tone is a decision, not a mood", "Live: prep a scene, together", "Close"], "handout": "**Prep and Atmosphere: the page and the three details.**\n\n**What you prepare decides what atmosphere you can deliver.**\n\n**The one-page sheet.** Six boxes, forty minutes, no more:\n\n1. Where they start. One line.\n2. Three names, one detail each; make the detail something you can see, hear, or smell.\n3. What the opposition wants.\n4. Two things that could go wrong.\n5. One thing they can find.\n6. How this probably ends, written knowing it will not.\n\n**Prep the things players will touch, not the things you want them to see.** Names, concrete specifics, and somebody who wants something all survive contact. Written dialogue, expected routes, and the clever twist do not.\n\n**Atmosphere is three details, then stop.** Pull from different senses; two visual details is one detail; the most atmospheric thing in a room is usually the thing that does not belong there.\n\n> \"The chapel smells like wet stone. Every saint along the wall has had their face scratched off. One of them is fresh.\"\n\n**Tone is a decision you make before the session.** It is carried by what you choose to describe, not by adjectives. If you have not decided it, your players will, and they will decide it is comedy.\n\n**This week:** fill in one sheet before your next session; forty minutes, not four hours.", "sheets": ["C2_Prep_Sheet.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "The join", "m": 5}, {"n": "What actually needs prepping", "m": 12}, {"n": "The one-page prep sheet", "m": 12}, {"n": "Atmosphere is three details, not a paragraph", "m": 12}, {"n": "Tone is a decision, not a mood", "m": 10}, {"n": "Live: prep a scene, together", "m": 18}, {"n": "Close", "m": 6}], "cuts": ["Subject 5 reduced to the bolded line plus the prep chime-in. (atmosphere)", "The reusable versus single-use bullets in subject 2. (T)", "Box 6 discussion in subject 3, keep the box on the sheet. (T)", "The volunteer readings in subject 6, down from three to one. (both)", "The scratch test walkthrough at the end of subject 4. (D)"], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 10. Subject 6 needs both teachers able to reach everyone in ten minutes.", "Cap at 10. Subject 6 needs both teachers able to reach everyone in ten minutes.", "The blank one-page sheet exists as C2_Prep_Sheet.pdf, one page, printable; release it at the start of subject 6 and again at the close. It now carries a one-word tone slot in box 1, which comes from T's chime-in in subject 5.", "Expect several attendees who have run games and come for the atmosphere half. Do not shorten the prep half for them; the spine is what they came for even if they do not know it."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap, and the blurb. The catalog blurb in section 3 of this document is what that blurb is for.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Read the code out, and paste the link in chat. Attendees need nothing else: no account, no install.", "Drive from the teacher view. Arrow keys move, a number jumps, b blacks the screen out for when attention should be on the conversation. The cue under each slide is the line from this brief.", "Release the handout at the end with the handout button, not as a file anyone has to find.", "End the class, then press *Log this run* and answer the three questions below; that is the after-action, and it lives in the room rather than in this document."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "C4": {"pub": {"id": "C4", "title": "Alternative Systems, Part 1: Popular Systems", "track": "DM 101", "level": "Practicum", "length": "90 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "many, by design", "prereq": "none. C19 is recommended, and C1 helps.", "blurb": "The same dragon is a tactical puzzle in one game, a death sentence in another, and a negotiation in a third, because the rules a table plays under shape the story it tells. For DMs choosing a system for a campaign, or wondering what the rest of the hobby is doing differently. You will leave able to read any system on sixteen categories and to match a campaign pitch to one, including what the table gives up by choosing it.", "floor": "They can take any system they have never seen, read what it rewards and what it makes costly, and say out loud which of two campaign pitches it is the better tool for and what the table gives up by choosing it.", "subjects": ["The rules shape the story", "Sixteen ways to read a system", "The reference point and its tactical cousin", "Horror and investigation", "Story-forward systems", "Tactical and heroic", "Gritty and deadly", "Live: match the pitch", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Alternative Systems, Part 1: Popular Systems.**\n\n**The rules a table plays under shape the story it tells, because rewards steer behaviour.** No system is best. Each one is a set of tradeoffs.\n\n**Three questions to ask of any system**, which is the sixteen categories compressed into something you can hold:\n\n1. What does it reward?\n2. What does it make costly?\n3. Who holds the story: the DM, the table, or the dice?\n\n**The nine rated categories.** Rules weight, lethality, roleplay support, tactical depth, power curve, prep load, new player friendliness, social and political depth, class balance and engagement.\n\n**The seven described ones.** Tone, story authority, campaign length fit, world interaction style, stats saves and resolution, signature mechanics, magic and key subsystems.\n\n**A rating describes a system's defaults.** A skilled table can push any game in another direction, and it pushes against the rules to do it.\n\n| System | In one line |\n|---|---|\n| D&D 5E | the shared reference point: moderate weight, high power curve, long campaigns |\n| Pathfinder 2E | the same ancestry with tactics and balance as the priority |\n| Call of Cthulhu | ordinary people, percentile skills, problems solved by learning |\n| Vampire 5E | dice pools, Hunger, and politics among the undead |\n| Daggerheart | Hope and Fear on two d12s, spotlight instead of initiative |\n| Blades in the Dark | position and effect, stress, and flashbacks after the job starts |\n| Draw Steel | heroes build resources, so fights accelerate |\n| Lancer | deep grid combat for the mechs, light rules for the pilots |\n| Cyberpunk RED | lethal firearms and lingering critical injuries |\n| Shadowdark | 5E shapes, far more danger, torches that burn in real time |\n| Stygian Depths | grimdark underdark; no spell slots, so magic is always available and always uncertain |\n\n**Lethal systems change how players think.** When death is near, players plan, scout, and avoid fights they cannot win. That caution is the design working.\n\n**This week:** take one system you have never played and write its three answers.", "sheets": []}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "The rules shape the story", "m": 5}, {"n": "Sixteen ways to read a system", "m": 12}, {"n": "The reference point and its tactical cousin", "m": 12}, {"n": "Horror and investigation", "m": 10}, {"n": "Story-forward systems", "m": 12}, {"n": "Tactical and heroic", "m": 10}, {"n": "Gritty and deadly", "m": 10}, {"n": "Live: match the pitch", "m": 14}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["Cosmere and Starfinder in subject 6, down to one line each.", "The build stage of subject 8. Run one pitch for the whole room, out loud.", "Fabula Ultima in subject 5, down to one line.", "The what-it-costs detail in subject 3, down to one line.", "Cyberpunk RED in subject 7, down to one line.", "The room check in subject 2."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 8 needs three or four working groups and a room small enough that three presentations fit in six minutes.", "One hard dependency: the profile cards, which are built from Kinspire_Alt_Systems_Master_List.md. The ratings are not filled in yet, so the cards do not exist and this class cannot run until they do.", "The profile cards are the spine. If a rating changes in the master list, the card changes with it, and the deck changes with the card.", "Say once, early, that Stygian Depths is yours. A worked example nobody is told the provenance of reads worse than one they are.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 8 survives by voice; the pitches get read out and the room matches one together."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Say in the posting that it is part one of two.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Check the current edition of anything you are going to name in the week before the run, not on the day.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "C4.1": {"pub": {"id": "C4.1", "title": "Alternative Systems, Part 2: Lesser-Known Systems", "track": "DM 101, module in C4", "level": "Practicum", "length": "90 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "many, by design", "prereq": "C4, or comfort with the sixteen categories and the profile card.", "blurb": "The popular systems do many things well, and a smaller system often does one thing exceptionally well, which is sometimes exactly what a campaign needs. For DMs who have taken part one, or who already read a system by what it rewards and what it costs, and want to know where to look when the familiar games do not fit. You will leave with a map of the wider hobby and one campaign of your own re-cast in a system you had not considered.", "floor": "They can name four families of system beyond the popular games and what each family is built to do, and take one campaign they would run in a familiar system and say what swapping it for a smaller one gains and what it costs.", "subjects": ["One thing done exceptionally well", "Old-school and gonzo", "Horror beyond Cthulhu", "The narrative families", "Toolkit systems", "Gentle, strange, and different", "Around the world", "The edges of the hobby", "Live: swap the system", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Alternative Systems, Part 2: Lesser-Known Systems.**\n\n**Somebody has usually already designed the game a DM keeps trying to homebrew.** The popular systems do many things well; a smaller system often does one thing exceptionally well.\n\n| Family | What it is built to do | Where to start |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Old-school and gonzo** | dangerous dungeons, light rules, rulings over rules | Old-School Essentials, Dungeon Crawl Classics, Mörk Borg |\n| **Horror beyond Cthulhu** | fear as a resource or a pressure in the rules | Mothership, Alien RPG, Trophy Dark |\n| **Powered by the Apocalypse** | genre-specific moves, and success with a cost | Monster of the Week, Masks, Dungeon World |\n| **Forged in the Dark** | position, effect and stress, in new settings | anything descended from Blades |\n| **Mythic powers** | characters as descriptive tags rather than numbers | City of Mist, Legend in the Mist |\n| **Toolkits** | one rulebook, many campaigns | GURPS, Savage Worlds, Fate Core |\n| **Gentle and strange** | tone chosen by the rules; sometimes no combat at all | Wanderhome, Ryuutama, The Wildsea |\n| **Outside the English market** | traditions that solved different problems | The Dark Eye, Tormenta20, Sword World |\n| **The edges** | solo play, GM-less play, one-shot rituals | Ironsworn, Microscope, Ten Candles |\n\n**The Powered by the Apocalypse roll, because the shape recurs everywhere.** Two six-sided dice plus a stat. High is a clean success, middle is success with a cost, low lets the DM make a move.\n\n**Narrative families share story authority with the players.** A DM used to holding the whole story will have to let go of part of it.\n\n**A toolkit saves learning time across campaigns and costs the sharp focus a dedicated game brings.**\n\n**Cozy games do not fail at being exciting; they succeed at something else.**\n\n**This week:** take one campaign you would run in a familiar system, name a smaller system that could carry it, and write the one thing the swap would cost you.", "sheets": []}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "One thing done exceptionally well", "m": 5}, {"n": "Old-school and gonzo", "m": 12}, {"n": "Horror beyond Cthulhu", "m": 12}, {"n": "The narrative families", "m": 12}, {"n": "Toolkit systems", "m": 8}, {"n": "Gentle, strange, and different", "m": 10}, {"n": "Around the world", "m": 8}, {"n": "The edges of the hobby", "m": 5}, {"n": "Live: swap the system", "m": 13}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["Subject 8 entire. Show the slide and move on.", "Three of the six games in subject 3, listed on the slide and not spoken.", "Cypher System and Genesys in subject 5.", "Household and The Wildsea in subject 6, keeping Wanderhome and Heart as the two ends.", "Ordem Paranormal and Sword World in subject 7.", "The build stage of subject 9. Run one swap for the whole room, out loud."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 9 needs three or four working groups and a room small enough that three swaps fit in five minutes.", "One hard dependency: Kinspire_Alt_Systems_Master_List.md, handed over at the close; the ratings are not filled in yet, so this class cannot run until they are.", "This is a lot of proper nouns in ninety minutes. The names are not the point and saying so early stops people taking notes they do not need.", "Subject 7's Sword World release date is unchecked. The two award claims in subject 4 were verified on 2026-10-24 and are safe to say.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 9 survives by voice; one pitch read out and swapped by the room together, which is also cut list item 6."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead. Say in the posting that C4 comes first, because a room arriving without the categories cannot do the practicum.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release the master list at the close rather than at the start. Handed out early it is something to read instead of listening to.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "C6": {"pub": {"id": "C6", "title": "Campaign Content Building and Tie-Ins", "track": "DM 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. C1 is recommended, and C2 pairs with it.", "blurb": "Five characters arrive carrying fifteen pieces of usable material before the DM has written anything, and most new DMs build a world beside it instead. For anybody about to start a campaign, or one or two sessions into one, who is not sure how much to prepare or what to attach it to. You will leave having taken one real character and turned something they already gave you into a situation that could open your next session.", "floor": "They can take one real character from their table, name five things that character already gives them, and turn one of those five into a situation that could open their next session.", "subjects": ["What you actually have to build", "The five things your players already gave you", "Turning one of them into a thing that happens", "What happens when they ignore it", "Threads, and how many to keep lit", "Live: one character, one thread", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Campaign Content Building and Tie-Ins.**\n\n**Most of your campaign already exists, and the players wrote it.**\n\n**You are not writing a campaign. You are writing the next three sessions and a direction.** Resolution falls off with distance: a situation for the next session, a sentence each for the two after, a direction for sessions four to ten, and a feeling beyond that.\n\n**Five things every character already gives you:**\n\n1. **A background.** A place they know, and people who know them.\n2. **Something they want.** A direction they will walk in without being pushed.\n3. **Somebody they care about.** A person who can be in trouble.\n4. **Something they are bad at.** A situation that costs them something.\n5. **A thing they carry.** An object with a history you did not have to invent.\n\n**Five characters means twenty five of these and you need about four.**\n\n**Turning one into something that happens, in four steps:**\n\n1. Take one given.\n2. Put a person on it, who wants something involving it.\n3. Put something in their way.\n4. Put it somewhere the party will be.\n\n> *Sefi is a criminal with a sister she sends money to. The person she used to work for has found the sister. He does not want Sefi dead, he wants her back, and he has three months of leverage. The letter arrives in the town the party is already travelling to.*\n\n**The step people skip is the third.** A person who wants something with nothing in their way is an errand.\n\n**When they ignore a thread:** leave it, move it to them, or let it resolve without them and let them see the result. **Do not escalate it until they have to care.**\n\n**Three to five threads lit at once.** Lit costs about one sentence a session; banked is a real state. Retire them out loud when they are done.\n\n**This week:** take one character from your table and run the four steps on one of their five givens.", "sheets": ["C6_Thread_Sheet.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "What you actually have to build", "m": 9}, {"n": "The five things your players already gave you", "m": 10}, {"n": "Turning one of them into a thing that happens", "m": 11}, {"n": "What happens when they ignore it", "m": 8}, {"n": "Threads, and how many to keep lit", "m": 8}, {"n": "Live: one character, one thread", "m": 10}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 4}], "cuts": ["The second worked conversion in subject 3, if it was run.", "The map problem bullet in subject 1.", "Subject 5's three thread examples, down to the first one.", "The character-with-nothing bullet in subject 2.", "The third option in subject 4, which is letting it resolve without them."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 6 collects two or three readings from a room that has all been writing, and above twelve that stops fitting in four minutes.", "One hard dependency: C6_Thread_Sheet.pdf, released in the arrival window; subject 6 is the sheet getting filled in.", "Have a real character sheet ready to put on screen for subject 2, either one of A1's six or one from your own table with the player's permission.", "Nobody is asked to share anything about a real person at their table. The characters are fictional and the class stays on them.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subjects 2 through 5 survive by voice; subject 6 needs the sheet, which is why it is released early."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb.", "Open the classroom and start this class. Five character room code, link to paste.", "Release the thread sheet in the arrival window, not at the start of subject 6.", "End the class in the classroom so the run log is written while you remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "C9": {"pub": {"id": "C9", "title": "Ethical DMing, Session 0, and Consent Management", "track": "DM 101", "level": "Open", "length": "75 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. C1 is recommended, and nothing here is gated.", "blurb": "Most material on safety at the table is a list of tools, and a tool nobody has ever used is a button rather than a policy. For anybody running a game who has announced a safety tool and never heard it used, and for anybody about to run their first session zero. You will leave having run the opening of one out loud, with a message you can send three months from now when the campaign has changed and the agreement has not.", "floor": "They can run the first ten minutes of a session zero out loud, name which tool answers which problem, and say how their table will raise something three months from now when the campaign has changed.", "subjects": ["The join", "What session zero is actually for", "The four tools, and when each one is used", "Running the conversation", "Consent after session zero", "Live: run the first ten minutes", "Close"], "handout": "**Ethical DMing, Session 0, and Consent Management.**\n\n**A tool is not a policy.** Having a card on the table does not make a table safe; it means there is a button. What makes it work is whether anybody has ever pressed it.\n\n**Session zero is not a safety meeting.** It is where the table agrees what game this is, and content is the fourth of four questions:\n\n1. What is this game about? Tone, genre, roughly what you will be doing.\n2. How does this table work? Attendance, lateness, what happens when somebody misses.\n3. What are we each here for? The thing each person wants, said out loud once.\n4. What is in and what is out? Content, and the tools for changing it later.\n\n**Reaching content fourth matters.** Opening on it reads as a warning; arriving at it reads as planning a game.\n\n**Four tools, and each one fails differently:**\n\n| The tool | What it answers | How it fails |\n|---|---|---|\n| Lines and veils | what is in the game at all | nobody remembers them two months later |\n| A card or a word | how do I stop this now, without explaining | it has never been used, so the first use costs too much |\n| Check-ins | how is this going, before anybody is in trouble | asked of the group, so nobody answers |\n| An open door | how do I say something I cannot say at the table | it is the DM's door, and sometimes the DM is the problem |\n\n**A line is something that does not happen in this game; a veil is something that can happen off screen without being described.**\n\n**Use the card yourself, on purpose, in the first three sessions.** Stop a scene of your own and rewind it. Ten seconds, and it turns a theory into a thing that has happened.\n\n**Ask check-ins of one person by name, between sessions.** A group question returns silence, and silence reads as consent to the asker and as pressure to everybody else.\n\n**Name a second adult who is not you**, because an open door that leads only to the DM is no route when the DM is the thing somebody wants to raise.\n\n**Go first.** Naming one of your own lines, small and without preamble, sets the price of naming one for everybody else. A DM who names nothing has said that naming things is unusual.\n\n**Consent set once is not consent.** The campaign changes, the people change, and new players arrive into an agreement they never made.\n\n**The re-ask, as a message, so it never becomes an occasion:**\n\n> \"We are about to start the next arc. Same question as session zero: anything you want off the table, or anything you have changed your mind about? Reply to me or in the group, either is fine.\"\n\n**When somebody uses a tool, the only correct response is to use it.** Stop, change the scene, carry on. No discussion at the table and no asking why.\n\n**When you get it wrong:** \"I took that further than I should have last week. I am not going back there; tell me if anything else in that session needs the same treatment.\"\n\n**This week:** name one of your own lines, out loud, in your next session zero.", "sheets": []}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "The join", "m": 5}, {"n": "What session zero is actually for", "m": 12}, {"n": "The four tools, and when each one is used", "m": 14}, {"n": "Running the conversation", "m": 12}, {"n": "Consent after session zero", "m": 12}, {"n": "Live: run the first ten minutes", "m": 14}, {"n": "Close", "m": 6}], "cuts": ["The new player problem at the end of subject 4.", "The second pass of the practicum in subject 6. One pass is the class.", "The three things that change in subject 5, down to the headline sentence.", "D's chime-in in subject 2.", "Subject 2's table down to the four questions as a spoken list."], "notes": ["The conduct line carries more weight in this class than in any other, because the subject is the thing the line is about. Say it slowly.", "Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 6 pairs people up, so an even number is better and the teacher makes a trio if it is odd.", "Nobody is asked for an example from their own table, in any subject. Say that in subject 1 and hold it. A room that expects to be asked will not speak at all.", "Expect at least one attendee who thinks safety tools are unnecessary, and at least one who has been at a table where one was needed and absent. Neither should be argued with. Subject 3's failure column is the answer to the first, and it works because it is a criticism of the tools rather than a defence of them.", "If anybody discloses something real in chat or by message, stop being a teacher. Thank them, take it privately, and tell whoever runs the program. Do not address it in the room.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subjects 2, 4 and 5 survive by voice alone."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap, and the blurb. The catalog blurb in section 3 of this document is what that blurb is for.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Read the code out, and paste the link in chat. Attendees need nothing else: no account, no install.", "Agree the clock before you start, and which of you is signalling.", "Drive from the teacher view. Arrow keys move, a number jumps, b blacks the screen out. The cue under each slide is the line from this brief, and the owner tag is on it.", "Black the screen out during subject 6. People are talking to each other, not reading.", "Release the handout at the end with the handout button.", "End the class, then press *Log this run* and answer the three questions below."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "C12": {"pub": {"id": "C12", "title": "Running Combat", "track": "DM 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "5E, adaptable in the room", "prereq": "none. C1 is recommended, and A10 is the player half of this hour.", "blurb": "Most bad fights are not badly balanced; they are badly paced, and the pacing is the part the DM holds. For people who have run a fight that dragged, or watched one get decided three rounds before it finished, and want the handful of habits that fix it. You will leave having run a round of combat out loud, and able to name the point at which a fight should have ended.", "floor": "They can run one round of combat out loud for three characters, say what each monster did and why it did that rather than attacking the nearest person, and name the point at which a fight they have run should have ended.", "subjects": ["What you are actually doing in a fight", "Initiative, and the admin that eats a fight", "Telegraph, so that decisions are possible", "Say what happened, not what the number was", "Knowing when the fight is over", "Live: two rounds, two volunteers", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Running Combat.**\n\n**Most bad fights are not badly balanced. They are badly paced**, and the pacing is the part you hold from the chair.\n\n**You touch a round four times, and only one of them is the monsters:**\n\n1. **At the top:** say whose turn it is, and whose is next.\n2. **During a turn:** answer one question, fast, and not more than one.\n3. **After a turn:** say what happened, in words.\n4. **At the end of the round:** decide whether there is another one.\n\n**The admin that eats a fight, and what to do instead.** One initiative roll for all the identical monsters. The order written down once, where you can see it. Two names called, not one. Hit points tracked in a column, counting down. No rolls for things that cannot matter.\n\n**Telegraph, so decisions are possible.** If a monster has something that will matter, let the table watch it happen before it matters to them. The cheap version is to describe what a monster is about to do one beat before it does it.\n\n**Say what happened, not what the number was.** What hit, where, and what it looks like now. Two sentences at most, and fewer when the round is dragging.\n\n> \"It gets inside your guard and the axe catches your shoulder. Seven. You are still up, and it is in your face now. Bren, you are next.\"\n\n**A fight is decided well before it ends.** When you notice the decision point, you have four ways to close it:\n\n| Close | When it fits |\n|---|---|\n| They run | almost always, and it is the most under-used |\n| They surrender | thinking enemies, and it makes the next hour |\n| You cut | when the outcome is not in doubt |\n| It ends properly | when the fight is close and it should be earned |\n\n**A fight that was over in round two and ran to round six is where a table learns that combat is boring.**\n\n**This week:** run one fight and write down which round the table knew how it would end.", "sheets": ["A10_Combat_Cards.pdf", "C12_Opposition.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "What you are actually doing in a fight", "m": 8}, {"n": "Initiative, and the admin that eats a fight", "m": 9}, {"n": "Telegraph, so that decisions are possible", "m": 9}, {"n": "Say what happened, not what the number was", "m": 8}, {"n": "Knowing when the fight is over", "m": 10}, {"n": "Live: two rounds, two volunteers", "m": 12}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 4}], "cuts": ["The second volunteer in subject 6. One round run properly is the class.", "The third worked telegraph in subject 3, which is the statue.", "Subject 2's two excerpt rounds, down to the ninety second one.", "The let-players-describe-their-hits bullet in subject 4.", "The counter-case in subject 5, down to its first sentence."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 10. Subject 6 needs four people in the fight and a room small enough that watching it is not passive.", "Two hard dependencies, both printables. C12_Opposition.pdf, which is new for this class, and A10_Combat_Cards.pdf, which already exists and is reused unchanged.", "Release both during the arrival window, so nobody is reading a card at minute forty.", "Decide in advance how the practicum fight starts, including where everybody is standing. A volunteer handed an unstarted fight spends their round building one.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice; the map is read out loud rather than drawn, the same as in A10.", "If the same person teaches A10. Running them in the same month, A10 first, is worth doing once to see whether the vocabulary actually carries."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead. Category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. The catalog blurb in section 3 is what that blurb is for.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release both printables in the arrival window, not at the start of subject 6.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "C15": {"pub": {"id": "C15", "title": "Resource Management", "track": "DM 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "5E, adaptable in the room", "prereq": "none. C1 is recommended.", "blurb": "Most tables that try resource management end up counting arrows that never run out, which is a chore with a theme rather than a pressure. For DMs who want the party to be weaker at the fourth fight than the first, and to have to decide what to do about it. You will leave with three resources chosen, an answer to how often your table can sleep, and the push-or-return decision run once out loud.", "floor": "They can name the three resources their own table will actually track, say how often their party can take a long rest and whether that is on purpose, and run the push-or-return decision once out loud.", "subjects": ["Bookkeeping against pressure", "The long rest is the whole variable", "What is actually scarce, and what is theatre", "Tell them the clock", "The push-or-return decision", "Live: one day, three decisions", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Resource Management.**\n\n**The point is not the count; the point is that they get weaker, and have to decide what to do about it.**\n\n**Counting a thing that never runs out is not resource management.** A resource can be untracked, tracked and never short, or tracked and sometimes short. Only the third one produces a decision.\n\n**The long rest is the whole variable.** Everything you track resets on one, so how often they can rest sets the ceiling on every other pressure.\n\n| Posture | What it costs them |\n|---|---|\n| Rest when you like | nothing |\n| Rest costs time, and time costs something | the thing that moved while they slept |\n| Rest is rare and conditional | the safety of stopping at all |\n\n**The middle row needs no rule change.** You do not restrict the rest; you make the world keep moving during it, using something they already knew about.\n\n**Pick three things and track those.** The load-bearing ones are spell slots, hit dice, and limited class features, because they run out in an afternoon. Rations and standard ammunition are mostly theatre. **The test: would you actually let them run out?**\n\n**Tell them the clock.** Say what is left, in rough units, unprompted; at the halves, at the last one, and before they spend something they may want later.\n\n> \"Last torch, and it is maybe half gone. The passage keeps going down.\"\n\n**The push-or-return decision needs three conditions at once:** they are weaker, there is more ahead, and going back costs something. Remove any one and the decision stops happening.\n\n**Retreat should cost something and not everything.** A DM who makes going back a failure teaches the party to push until somebody dies.\n\n**This week:** write down your three resources, and find out how often your party can actually take a long rest.", "sheets": ["C15_The_Day.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "Bookkeeping against pressure", "m": 8}, {"n": "The long rest is the whole variable", "m": 10}, {"n": "What is actually scarce, and what is theatre", "m": 9}, {"n": "Tell them the clock", "m": 8}, {"n": "The push-or-return decision", "m": 10}, {"n": "Live: one day, three decisions", "m": 11}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 4}], "cuts": ["The third decision point in subject 6. Two is enough to show the shape.", "The worked set in subject 3, which is the cleric-and-road example.", "Subject 4's three places to say it, down to the half rule.", "The hit dice bullet in subject 3.", "The counter-case in subject 5, down to its first sentence."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. The practicum is the whole room deciding together, so it tolerates more people than C12 does.", "One hard dependency: C15_The_Day.pdf, which is new for this class; release it during the arrival window, so nobody is reading it at minute forty.", "Decide before the run which of the three stops is the close one. The sheet suggests the second. A room that rests early can move it.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 6 survives by voice. The day is read out rather than shown, and the resource state gets said twice instead of once.", "If the same person teaches C12. Running them in the same month, C15 second, gives a DM the day and then the fight inside it. The handoff line, if both are in one schedule: \"C12 was one fight; this is the four of them in a row.\""], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. The catalog blurb in section 3 is what that blurb is for.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release the printable in the arrival window, not at the start of subject 6.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "C17": {"pub": {"id": "C17", "title": "Ruling on the Fly", "track": "DM 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "5E", "prereq": "none. C1 and C19 are recommended, not required.", "blurb": "Every rulebook has holes in it, and the game happens at the holes. For anybody who has frozen mid-session with a player waiting and a book open, and anybody who has invented something on the spot and regretted it the following week. You will leave able to give a ruling with a roll, a number and a stake in it, in one sentence and in under five seconds, having done it out loud a dozen times.", "floor": "They can make a ruling out loud in under five seconds, say it in a shape the table can act on, and hold it to the end of the session without reopening it.", "subjects": ["The hole in the map", "The four seconds", "The shape of a good ruling", "Adjudicating by analogy", "Live: rulings on the clock", "Holding it, and reopening", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Ruling on the Fly.**\n\n**The rules are a map with holes in it.** At a hole your job is a ruling that fits the map, not one that is correct.\n\n**Three kinds of hole:** no rule at all, two rules that disagree, and a rule that exists and is wrong here. Most of what feels like a missing rule is the third.\n\n**The window is about four seconds.** Past it the table stops waiting and starts talking, and getting them back costs more than the ruling was worth.\n\n**A fast ruling that is wrong costs one moment. A slow ruling that is right costs the room's attention,** and attention does not come back on request.\n\n**A ruling the table can act on has three parts, and all three fit in one sentence:** what you are rolling, what you are trying to beat, and what happens if you do not.\n\n> Not a ruling: \"Sure, roll for it.\"\n>\n> A ruling: \"Give me an Acrobatics check, fifteen or better. Miss it and you land in the middle of them instead of behind them.\"\n\n**Name the stake before the roll.** After the roll it is a punishment; before the roll it is a choice.\n\n**Find the nearest rule that does exist, and borrow its shape.** You are extending a rule rather than inventing one. Two questions find it in about two seconds: what is this most like that the game already handles, and who is resisting.\n\n**Swinging is jumping. Tipping something onto somebody is shoving; holding a door is a repeated saving throw.**\n\n**If no analogy comes in two seconds, pick a number.** Easy is 10, normal is 15, hard is 20.\n\n**A ruling holds until the end of the session.** Inside a session, consistency beats correctness.\n\n**Between sessions is when you fix it, out loud:** \"Last week I ruled that as a Strength check. I looked it up; it is a contest; we will do it that way from now on.\"\n\n**This week:** make one ruling with all three parts in it, and notice whether anybody asks a follow-up question.", "sheets": []}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "The hole in the map", "m": 8}, {"n": "The four seconds", "m": 8}, {"n": "The shape of a good ruling", "m": 11}, {"n": "Adjudicating by analogy", "m": 9}, {"n": "Live: rulings on the clock", "m": 16}, {"n": "Holding it, and reopening", "m": 4}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 4}], "cuts": ["The second pass of the practicum in subject 5. One pass is the class.", "The bad ruling demonstration in subject 3, keep the good one.", "Situations 9 and 11 in subject 5.", "The three kinds of hole in subject 1, down to the headline sentence.", "Subject 6 reduced to the scripted correction line."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 5 names one person at a time, and twelve rulings at ten seconds plus reaction time is already most of a pass.", "Nothing needs to exist in the world before this runs. No book, no sheet, no pre-made characters.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 5 is the one that survives by voice alone; move to it and run both passes.", "Nobody is corrected in subject 5. Say that before the first situation, because a room that expects to be marked will take eleven seconds thinking instead of ten seconds ruling.", "The twelve situations exist as a printed card, C17_Situations.pdf, one page, so the teacher is reading from paper rather than from the slide they are also driving. The slide shows the room the situation; the card shows the teacher the whole list and the two deliberate ones."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap, and the blurb. The catalog blurb in section 3 of this document is what that blurb is for.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Read the code out, and paste the link in chat. Attendees need nothing else: no account, no install.", "Drive from the teacher view. Arrow keys move, a number jumps, b blacks the screen out; the cue under each slide is the line from this brief.", "Leave each situation on screen during subject 5, because a person ruling needs to see it while they think.", "Release the handout at the end with the handout button.", "End the class, then press *Log this run* and answer the three questions below."], "answers": "", "sheets": ["C17_Situations.pdf"]}}, "C19": {"pub": {"id": "C19", "title": "Rules: What They Are For", "track": "DM 101", "level": "Open", "length": "60 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "5E", "prereq": "none. C1 is recommended, not required.", "blurb": "A rulebook reads as nine hundred things to memorize, and it is actually six rules you know, about fifteen you look up, and a pile of reference. For anybody running a game who has stalled mid-session with a book open, and anybody who has not started because of how thick it is. You will leave able to say what job a rule is doing, with the short list of the ones that come up, and having found four of them in under thirty seconds each.", "floor": "They can say what job a given rule is doing at the table, name the handful they will look up in a real session, and find one of them in under thirty seconds.", "subjects": ["What a rule is doing there", "Three tiers of the book", "The fifteen you will actually look up", "Live: find a rule in thirty seconds", "Who the rule is protecting", "When a rule is wrong for your table", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Rules: What They Are For.**\n\n**Every rule answers a question somebody got tired of arguing about.** Knowing which question tells you when to reach for it and when to leave it closed.\n\n**Four jobs. Nearly every rule is doing one of them:**\n\n| The job | Example |\n|---|---|\n| Resolve uncertainty | an ability check |\n| Distribute authority | the DM sets the number |\n| Pace the table | a turn, a round, an action |\n| Protect a player | death saving throws |\n\n**The book is three documents bound together.** Six rules are load-bearing and need no book. About fifteen are look-up; everything else is reference you read when you want to, never at the table.\n\n**The fifteen you will actually look up:** death saving throws, conditions, grappling and shoving, cover, hiding, surprise and initiative, opportunity attacks, concentration, resting, falling, suffocating, carrying capacity, improvised damage, exhaustion, jumping and climbing.\n\nConditions, hiding and opportunity attacks generate more argument than the rest combined.\n\n**Finding one fast:** search beats the index whenever you know the word, and most questions are a word you know. **Say the search term out loud before you search it.** \"Shove\", not \"beam\"; \"concentration\", not \"the wizard got hit\".\n\n**Set three to five bookmarks before the session, not during:** conditions, death saving throws, the combat actions list, resting, and whatever your party keeps doing.\n\n**Thirty seconds is the target; past a minute, stop looking and make a ruling.**\n\n**Ask who a rule is protecting.** Most rules that feel restrictive to a DM are protecting a player. Most that feel restrictive to a player are protecting the table's time.\n\n**Consistency is what players want, more than correctness.** A table that can predict you can plan; a table that cannot stops trying.\n\n**\"I do not know this rule\" and \"this rule is wrong for us\" feel identical and are not.** If you can name the job the rule was doing and still think it is wrong, that is a real objection. Park it until between sessions.\n\n**This week:** set your bookmarks, and look up hiding before somebody makes you.", "sheets": []}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "What a rule is doing there", "m": 9}, {"n": "Three tiers of the book", "m": 8}, {"n": "The fifteen you will actually look up", "m": 10}, {"n": "Live: find a rule in thirty seconds", "m": 14}, {"n": "Who the rule is protecting", "m": 8}, {"n": "When a rule is wrong for your table", "m": 7}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 4}], "cuts": ["Situation 4 in the drill. Keep the first three.", "The \"rule of cool\" bullets in subject 5. Keep the three illustrations.", "Subject 1's live exercise. Keep the four-job table.", "Rows 11 to 15 of the fifteen, read as \"and a handful more, in the handout\".", "Subject 2's third tier discussion. Keep the table."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Post the link to the free 5E rules in chat in the arrival window. Subject 4 does not work without it, and people arriving at minute nine need time to open it.", "The link is confirmed as of 2026-10-17 and lives in the teacher card rather than here, so one line changes rather than two when it moves.", "Seat cap 14. The drill scales better than a writing practicum, since calling out a page is fast.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subjects 1, 2 and 5 survive by voice alone; move to them and run the drill at the end if the build returns.", "A teacher should have their own bookmarks already set, visible, as the subject 4 demonstration; setting them live is more convincing than describing them."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap, and the blurb. The catalog blurb in section 3 of this document is what that blurb is for.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Read the code out, and paste the link in chat, along with the rules link.", "Drive from the teacher view. Arrow keys move, a number jumps, b blacks the screen out; the cue under each slide is the line from this brief.", "Black the screen out during each drill situation. They should be looking at the rules, not at a slide.", "Release the handout at the end with the handout button.", "End the class, then press *Log this run* and answer the three questions below."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "C19.1": {"pub": {"id": "C19.1", "title": "Finding a Rule Fast", "track": "DM 101, module in C19", "level": "Open", "length": "30 minutes, plus a 5 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "5E", "prereq": "C19 strongly recommended. It is the parent class and this assumes its subject 4.", "blurb": "A half hour of setup that makes every rules lookup afterwards faster, which is most of what looking fast at the table actually is. For anybody who runs a game and has lost four minutes to a book, and for anybody who took C19 and wants the preparation half of it. You will leave with your bookmarks set, a one page reference of the three things your table looks up most, and somewhere to keep the rulings you have already made.", "floor": "They leave with their own bookmarks set, a one page reference holding the three things their table looks up most, and a place to write the rulings their table has already made.", "subjects": ["What this is, and what it is not", "Bookmarks for your table, not a generic five", "The three pages you lift out of the book", "The page that is not in any book", "Live: set yours now", "Close"], "handout": "**Finding a Rule Fast.**\n\n**The fastest lookup is the one you did last week.** Most of the speed in a thirty second lookup was bought in advance.\n\n**Five bookmarks, chosen for your table.** Conditions, death saving throws, the combat actions list, resting, and whatever your party keeps doing. **The fifth is the one that matters.** Think back over your last three sessions and name the three things you looked up; those are probably your real five. Name them in plain words rather than by page number, and five is a real cap.\n\n**Three pages lifted out of the book.** Conditions, the combat actions list, and death saving throws, for most tables. Type them out yourself, keep it to one page, and put it somewhere that does not require a search.\n\n**The page that is not in any book.** Three columns: the date, what somebody tried, and what you decided.\n\n> *2026-11-02. Climbing a rope while holding a torch. Athletics at 10, and the torch goes out on a failure.*\n\nWrite it during the session or straight after. **A ruling that comes back three times is a house rule**, and that is D29's process rather than a log.\n\n**Where this sits.** C19 is the drill. C17 is when the lookup fails and you rule instead. D29 is the ruling that became permanent.\n\n**This week:** use the bookmarks once, and write one line on the rulings log.", "sheets": ["C19.1_Setup_Sheet.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "What this is, and what it is not", "m": 4}, {"n": "Bookmarks for your table, not a generic five", "m": 7}, {"n": "The three pages you lift out of the book", "m": 7}, {"n": "The page that is not in any book", "m": 6}, {"n": "Live: set yours now", "m": 5}, {"n": "Close", "m": 1}], "cuts": ["The second worked example in subject 4.", "The where-it-lives bullet in subject 3.", "Subject 2's method, down to its first sentence.", "The early-finisher question in subject 5.", "Subject 4 entire, down to the three columns read aloud. This is the last resort and it loses the module's second best idea."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first minute.", "Seat cap 14. Nothing here is paired or collected, so the cap is about the room rather than the exercise, and it matches C19.", "One hard dependency: C19.1_Setup_Sheet.pdf, released in the arrival window. The module is the sheet getting filled in.", "The teacher needs their own bookmarks unset at the start of subject 2, or set them again live. Watching somebody do it is the demonstration.", "The free rules link goes in chat during the arrival window, the same one C19 uses, so nobody is hunting for a book.", "Thirty minutes moves fast and subject 5 is the one that gets squeezed. Start it at the twenty four minute mark whatever is unfinished."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board. It is a module, so it appears under C19 in the class dropdown and is marked as one.", "Open the classroom and start this class. Five character room code, link to paste.", "Release the setup sheet and the free rules link in the arrival window.", "End the class in the classroom so the run log is written while you remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "D2": {"pub": {"id": "D2", "title": "Ethics of DM Management", "track": "DM 102", "level": "Seated", "length": "75 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral, with one 5E-specific passage in subject 5", "prereq": "none. C9 is strongly recommended, and C1 is recommended.", "blurb": "A DM holds four different kinds of authority, they arrived by four different routes, and most of what gets called a DM ethics problem is one of them being used to do another one's job. For people already running a table who want to name what they are holding rather than argue about how much of it they should have. You will leave able to name the four, say which one a real decision of yours used, and say the three things you are going to tell your table before the next session.", "floor": "They can name the four kinds of authority they hold at their own table, say which one they used for a specific decision last session, and say the three things they are going to tell their table out loud before the next one.", "subjects": ["The join", "The four powers", "What each one is for, and how each goes wrong", "Said out loud, once", "The dice behind the screen", "Getting it wrong, and the repair", "Live: name the power you used", "Close"], "handout": "**Ethics of DM Management.**\n\n**The chair is lent.** Every power you hold was handed to you one at a time by people who can hand it back, and the only one that behaves like property is the one you invented.\n\n**You are holding four different things:**\n\n| Power | What it is for | What it looks like when borrowed |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Narrative** | a world that responds to what people do | the fiction punishing a player for something that happened between people |\n| **Procedural** | resolving what nobody knows the answer to | the difficulty moving because you already know what should happen |\n| **Social** | keeping an evening moving for everybody | airtime handed out as a reward and withheld as a correction |\n| **Administrative** | keeping a table that can actually meet | somebody removed for being inconvenient rather than for doing harm |\n\n**The right-hand column is one thing four times.** A power being used to deliver an outcome that belongs to a conversation. The repair is the same every time: say the thing instead of routing it through the game.\n\n**Three things to say to your table once, early, and then not again:**\n\n1. **How death works here.** Whether a character can die, and whether you will stop it.\n2. **Whether you roll in the open.** And if not, what you do with a result you did not want.\n3. **What happens when you and the table read a rule differently mid-session.**\n\n**On rolling in the open, the class takes no side.** Both are defensible and plenty of good tables run each way. What is harder to defend is running one and describing the other.\n\n**When you get it wrong, the repair is short:** name it, say what you are doing instead, and move.\n\n> \"I got that one wrong last week. The rule is X, we are using X from here, and the thing it cost you I am giving back this way.\"\n\n**Twice is a pattern**, and the table noticed before you did.\n\n**This week:** write the three sentences and send them to your table.", "sheets": []}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "The join", "m": 5}, {"n": "The four powers", "m": 12}, {"n": "What each one is for, and how each goes wrong", "m": 12}, {"n": "Said out loud, once", "m": 9}, {"n": "The dice behind the screen", "m": 10}, {"n": "Getting it wrong, and the repair", "m": 10}, {"n": "Live: name the power you used", "m": 12}, {"n": "Close", "m": 5}], "cuts": ["The second round of the practicum in subject 7, if it was added. One round is the class.", "The fourth row of the misuse table in subject 3, down to the headline sentence.", "Subject 6's three shapes, down to the repair script alone.", "D's chime-in in subject 2.", "Subject 5's two tables, down to the two sentences in the excerpt."], "notes": ["Seat cap 10. Subject 7 pairs people up and then collects in the room, and above ten the collection stops fitting in four minutes.", "The Discord fallback line, said in the first two minutes.", "Nothing needs to exist in the world before this runs. No handout is released until the close.", "If somebody describes a situation that is actually D17's, which is a person rather than a decision, say so and route it rather than running it in the room.", "If anybody discloses something real about their own table that belongs in private, stop being a teacher, thank them, take it privately, and tell whoever runs the program. Same rule as C9.", "Subject 5 will produce a disagreement in the room at least once. That is the subject working. Let two people state positions, do not referee it, and move on by the clock.", "The arrival window is ten minutes and this class needs all of it, because subject 1 asks who has taken C9.", "Both teachers should have their own answer to subject 5 ready in one sentence. The room will ask, and two teachers hedging reads worse than two teachers disagreeing."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Say in the posting that it is co-taught and that it follows C9.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Release the handout at the close rather than the start. Subject 7 is better if people have not read the four powers as a list first.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "D4": {"pub": {"id": "D4", "title": "Build Tension, Psychological Horror, and Psychological Engagement", "track": "DM 102", "level": "Seated", "length": "75 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "18+", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "none. C2 and C9 are both recommended, and C9 matters more here than anywhere else in the catalog.", "blurb": "Most frightening moments at a table were a sound, a wrong detail, or a clock running down rather than a monster reveal, which means fear is built rather than described. For DMs who want to run horror and want the routine that keeps a frightened table a safe one. You will leave with a three beat dread sequence for an ordinary place and a safety routine for before, during, and after a session.", "floor": "They can name the difference between tension, dread, and horror, build a three beat dread sequence for an ordinary place, and say what they will do before, during, and after a session to keep a frightened table a safe one.", "subjects": ["The table chooses to be afraid", "Tension, dread, and horror", "Building tension", "Dread: show the signs before the thing", "Psychological horror techniques", "Afraid but able to act", "Horror and safety", "Live: a dread sequence in three beats", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Build Tension, Psychological Horror, and Psychological Engagement.**\n\n**A DM does not frighten players.** A DM builds the conditions in which players frighten themselves, with their agreement, and keeps those conditions safe.\n\n| Word | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| Tension | uncertainty about an outcome that matters |\n| Dread | anticipation of a threat the players already sense |\n| Horror | the moment something violates what was believed safe, normal, or true |\n\n**A reveal with no dread behind it lands as a fact rather than as a fear.**\n\n**Three tension tools.** A visible clock the players can watch running down; a scarce resource, so that every use is a decision. Withheld information, because the gap between noticing and knowing is where tension lives.\n\n**Escalate the signs rather than the volume.** Something is wrong somewhere, then something is wrong here, then something is wrong with us. Name a detail and stop; the table will build something worse than you can describe.\n\n**Six techniques, all aimed at the character.** Wrongness in the familiar; unreliable perception, used sparingly. Isolation, kept short. Corrupted safety, used once; bonds as stakes, with the player's agreement; the slow cost, which stays.\n\n**The test for whether it is still horror:** at any frightening moment, can the players name two things they could try? If not, it has become a cutscene.\n\n**Unbroken fear goes numb.** Build a rhythm of pressure and relief.\n\n**The safety routine, in three parts.** Before: lines and veils agreed in session zero; during: a tool anybody can use to pause or remove content, honoured at once and without questions. After: a two minute check-in on what landed and what went too far.\n\n**A frightened character is the goal; a distressed player is a signal to stop.**\n\n**This week:** take one ordinary place your party will visit and write two Omens for it.", "sheets": []}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "The table chooses to be afraid", "m": 5}, {"n": "Tension, dread, and horror", "m": 8}, {"n": "Building tension", "m": 10}, {"n": "Dread: show the signs before the thing", "m": 10}, {"n": "Psychological horror techniques", "m": 10}, {"n": "Afraid but able to act", "m": 8}, {"n": "Horror and safety", "m": 9}, {"n": "Live: a dread sequence in three beats", "m": 12}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 3}], "cuts": ["The build stage of subject 8. Run one sequence together, out loud, in five minutes.", "The pacing list in subject 3, down to its first line.", "The farmer in subject 4, keeping the three step escalation.", "Two of the six techniques in subject 5, left on the slide and not spoken.", "The room check in subject 2, giving the answers directly instead."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 8 needs three or four working groups and a room small enough that two readings fit in five minutes.", "No hard dependency on a printable. The handout is a takeaway rather than something released in the arrival window.", "Decide in advance how you will handle a real disclosure in subject 1. It is the one moment in this class where the room can go somewhere nobody planned.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subject 8 survives by voice; groups become one room building one sequence, which is also cut list item 1."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb. Mark the 18+ rating where it is visible before anybody takes a seat.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Say the conduct line in the arrival window, not at the start of subject 1.", "End the class in the classroom rather than just leaving, so the run log is written while you still remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "D5": {"pub": {"id": "D5", "title": "Advanced Campaign Content, Arcs, and Tie-Ins", "track": "DM 102", "level": "Seated", "length": "75 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "system-neutral", "prereq": "C6 strongly recommended. This assumes a campaign already running.", "blurb": "A long campaign that feels planned almost never was; what happened is that the DM kept tying new things to what had already happened. For anybody running a campaign past its first few sessions who wants arcs that survive the table doing something unexpected. You will leave able to name something you planted without meaning to, and one connection you could draw backwards between two things that have already happened.", "floor": "They can name one thing they planted in their own campaign without meaning to, say what it could pay off into, and describe one connection they could draw backwards between two things that have already happened at their table.", "subjects": ["The join", "What an arc actually is", "Planting: what survives thirty sessions", "Coherence is made backwards", "A player's thread becoming the main thread", "When the direction changes", "Live: connect two things", "Close"], "handout": "**Advanced Campaign Content, Arcs, and Tie-Ins.**\n\n**Coherence is made backwards.** A long campaign that feels planned almost never was; the DM kept tying new things to what had already happened.\n\n**An arc is not a plot you are delivering. It is a pressure that changes.** If it can be described without mentioning the players, it is a plot.\n\n| A plot | An arc |\n|---|---|\n| the cult summons the thing in session twelve | the cult is recruiting, and it is getting easier for them |\n| the baron betrays them at the bridge | the baron is running out of money, and he has three ways out |\n\n**Two or three arcs at once is the ceiling.**\n\n**A plant is a detail you put in with no plan for it.** It costs one sentence and most of them never pay. To be usable twenty sessions later it has to be **concrete**, **unexplained**, and **witnessed**.\n\n**Connecting backwards, in three steps:**\n\n1. List four things that have happened and are still unexplained.\n2. Pick two that could share a cause; not the two that obviously go together.\n3. Decide what the cause is, and let the table find it.\n\n**Is it cheating?** No; you are deciding what was true, which is the job. You would be over the line if you changed what the table saw happen, or retold an event differently from how it ran.\n\n**When one player's thread becomes the campaign:** check spotlight over five sessions, not over one. If one name drove three or more, connect the next thing to somebody else, and ask the quiet player what they want in.\n\n**When the direction changes**, keep the people they know by name, the pressures still pushing, the things planted and witnessed, and what the campaign turned out to be about. Let go of the places they never went and the plot for the place they left.\n\n**This week:** start a planting log, and write one connection you could draw backwards.", "sheets": ["D5_Planting_Log.pdf"]}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "The join", "m": 5}, {"n": "What an arc actually is", "m": 13}, {"n": "Planting: what survives thirty sessions", "m": 12}, {"n": "Coherence is made backwards", "m": 13}, {"n": "A player's thread becoming the main thread", "m": 10}, {"n": "When the direction changes", "m": 10}, {"n": "Live: connect two things", "m": 8}, {"n": "Close", "m": 4}], "cuts": ["The second reading in subject 7.", "The three to one rule in subject 3, down to its first sentence.", "Subject 6's say-it-out-loud bullet.", "T's chime-in in subject 4.", "Subject 5's rough check, down to the spotlight-over-sessions line."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 7 collects two readings from a room that has all been writing, and above twelve that stops fitting in two minutes.", "One hard dependency: D5_Planting_Log.pdf, released in the arrival window; subject 3 ends with people starting one.", "Have four unexplained things from your own campaign ready for anybody with no campaign running; they go on the practicum slide and they should be real ones.", "Nobody is asked about the people at their table, only about the fiction. Subject 5 is about spotlight rather than about a person, and if somebody brings a person, that is D17 and it gets routed.", "If the build breaks mid-class, everything except subject 7 survives by voice, and subject 7 survives if the log was released early."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap and the blurb.", "Open the classroom and start this class. Five character room code, link to paste.", "Release the planting log in the arrival window.", "End the class in the classroom so the run log is written while you remember it."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}, "D29": {"pub": {"id": "D29", "title": "Homebrew Rules", "track": "DM 102", "level": "Seated", "length": "75 minutes, plus a 10 minute arrival window", "ages": "all ages", "system": "5E", "prereq": "none. C19 and C17 are strongly recommended, and the reason is in the spine.", "blurb": "Most house rules are aimed at the wrong complaint and checked against nothing, which is how a table ends up with a player who is quietly having less fun and cannot say why. For anybody already running a game who has changed a rule, or is about to. You will leave having run one of your own rules through a three question check, written it in four lines, and said in advance what would make you take it back.", "floor": "They can take one rule they want to change, say what complaint it answers, name what else in the game touches it, write it in one sentence, and say in advance what would make them take it back.", "subjects": ["Every house rule is a complaint", "What people change, and what it touches", "The blast radius, live", "Writing it so it survives", "Testing on a live table", "Retiring one", "Where to go next"], "handout": "**Homebrew Rules.**\n\n**Every house rule is a complaint.** Fix the complaint, not the rule you happened to be looking at. \"Combat drags\" is usually turn length rather than monster health; \"we never get to use our abilities\" is usually too few encounters between rests.\n\n**The dangerous changes are the ones that touch something a player chose.** A player who took Brutal Critical chose it. A player who took the Healer feat chose it; the safe changes touch things nobody built a character around.\n\n**Change how long a rest is and you have changed which classes are good,** and nobody will connect it to the rest rule three weeks later.\n\n**The blast radius, three questions, about two minutes:**\n\n1. Which class features assume the old rule?\n2. Which monsters or encounters assume it?\n3. What does it look like at level 11, not level 3?\n\n**Question 3 catches the most and is skipped the most,** because almost all homebrew is written while running a low level game.\n\n**Write it in four lines:** what changed, the complaint it answers, the date it started, and what would make you take it back. The fourth line is the one nobody writes and the one that makes retiring possible.\n\n**Five house rules is a lot; ten and nobody remembers them, including you.**\n\n**Test it over three sessions.** One is the novelty, two is the adjustment, three is how it plays. Ask one question at the end of each: did that change anything for you tonight? Not \"do you like it,\" because liking is a vote and this is not a vote.\n\n**Three failure signatures:** it quietly makes one character worse, nobody can remember it, or it only ever helps the DM.\n\n**The first is the serious one.** A player whose character got worse rarely says so, because saying so means claiming you hurt them on purpose. Go and ask them directly and privately.\n\n**Taking one back, out loud:** \"I added the flanking rule six weeks ago to make positioning matter. It has mostly made the rogue's sneak attack free, which was not the point, so we are dropping it from next session. Positioning still matters, I will handle it in description.\"\n\n**A rule that was retired is not a failure.** Three sessions of data is the point of a test.\n\n**This week:** take one rule you already use, write the four lines for it, and ask question two.", "sheets": []}, "tea": {"timings": [{"n": "Every house rule is a complaint", "m": 9}, {"n": "What people change, and what it touches", "m": 12}, {"n": "The blast radius, live", "m": 14}, {"n": "Writing it so it survives", "m": 9}, {"n": "Testing on a live table", "m": 12}, {"n": "Retiring one", "m": 13}, {"n": "Where to go next", "m": 6}], "cuts": ["The six-row table in subject 2, down to the rest row and the inspiration row.", "The fourth and fifth attendee rules in subject 3's practicum.", "Subject 5's per-session note-taking bullets, keep the three sessions and the one question.", "The second and third failure signatures in subject 6, keep the first and the script.", "Subject 4's date and location bullets, keep the four-line shape."], "notes": ["Discord fallback link in the first two minutes.", "Seat cap 12. Subject 3 takes four or five attendee rules out loud, and above twelve the people who do not get picked have sat through a practicum without doing it.", "Ask in the arrival window for one house rule each, used or wanted, so subject 3 is not starting from a blank room. Somebody with none can borrow one from the table in subject 2.", "Expect at least one attendee who came to have a specific rule approved. Subject 3 is the answer: the questions do the judging and the teacher does not.", "Expect disagreement about the examples, and let it happen. Several of them are contested and a DM track at 102 that nobody argues in is not pitched right.", "If the build breaks mid-class, subjects 1, 3 and 6 survive by voice alone. Move to them."], "day": ["Post it on the notice board first, days ahead; category, then level, then the class, then the date, the cap, and the blurb. The catalog blurb in section 3 of this document is what that blurb is for.", "On the day, open the classroom and start this class. You get a five-character room code and a link to paste.", "Read the code out, and paste the link in chat. Attendees need nothing else: no account, no install.", "Ask for their one house rule in chat during the arrival window, so subject 3 has material waiting.", "Drive from the teacher view. Arrow keys move, a number jumps, b blacks the screen out; the cue under each slide is the line from this brief.", "Leave the three questions on screen through subject 3. People answering them need to see them.", "Release the handout at the end with the handout button.", "End the class, then press *Log this run* and answer the three questions below."], "answers": "", "sheets": []}}};
